to protest, and was gently pushed back by Pyle. \"Miss Francis, I'd like to ask you a particular question \u2014 you may find it quite strange,\" said the policeman, leaning back in his chair. \"From the smell of whisky on his breath, can you tell if it was good whisky or cheap whisky? \u2026 You see! I knew you'd laugh.\" Everyone chuckled at the strange expression on Quinn's face. [1274] \"Well, officer \u2014 this question is quite difficult to answer,\" said the girl candidly. \"I'm afraid I'm not very familiar with whisky. But in my memory, that smell should be coming from good whisky. Good whisky \u2014 but too much of it!\" She shook her head sadly. [1275] \"If I were there, I'd be able to tell you what whisky it was right away,\" muttered Stanford Evans-Pope. [1276] His father's lips were tightly closed, but soon relaxed into a smile. He shook his head at Stanford, warning him. [1277] \"Go on, Miss Francis,\" the officer said. [1278] \"I was so scared then,\" the girl admitted, her red lips still trembling, \"and felt sick - I broke free from his hands and stumbled into the theater. I remember sitting down, hearing the warning bell for the second act. I can't remember how I got back to my seat. My heart was in my throat. I remember thinking then that I couldn't tell Stephen - Mr. Barry - about this, worried he'd go after that man. You know, Mr. Barry is quite jealous.\" [1279] \"That's all I remember happening on Monday night, officer,\" she continued, \"I know you'll ask me how my wallet ended up with him. Well - I don't know, officer. I swear on my honor, I don't remember anything about the wallet.\" [1280] Quin shifted in his chair. \"What's this, Miss Francis?\" [1281] \"In fact, I didn't realize I had lost it until you showed it to me in the manager's office,\" she answered bravely, \"I remember taking it with me to the bathroom after the first act ended, and leaving it open, using my powder puff. But whether I left it in the bathroom or dropped it somewhere else later, I don't know.\" [1282] \"Did you ever think, Miss Francis,\" Quinn interrupted her, reaching for his snuff box and then putting it back in his pocket, embarrassed by Mrs. Eves-Pope's cold stare, \"that perhaps the gentleman came to bother you and dropped it in the alley?\" [1283] The girl's face lit up with relief. \"Yes, officer!\" she exclaimed, \"I've always thought so, but this explanation sounds lame - I was too afraid to explain it to you like that! I really can't remember, does this explanation seem logical to you? - when he grabbed my wrist, I dropped my wallet on the ground, and then completely forgot about it.\" [1284] The officer smiled. \"On the contrary, my dear,\" he said, \"this is the only explanation that makes sense of the whole thing. He probably found your wallet there - picked it up - in a half-drunk, half-awake state, out of love, he put it in his pocket, probably intending to return it to you later. That way, he would have had the opportunity to see you again. He seems to have been completely infatuated with you, my dear - and that's no wonder.\" [1285] \"Now - one more thing, Miss Francis, and this investigation will be over,\" Quinn continued, \"can you describe his appearance?\" [1286] \"Oh, of course!\" Miss Francis replied quickly. \"He made a deep impression on me, you can imagine. He's a little taller than me\u2014around five feet eight inches\u2014a bit overweight. His face is puffy, with two deep, pale gray bags under his eyes. I've never seen such a debauched person. He shaved his beard clean. Apart from his prominent nose, there's nothing remarkable about him.\" [1287] \"That should be our friend, Monty Field,\" the officer said firmly. \"Now\u2014think carefully, Miss Francis, have you ever seen him anywhere else\u2014do you know him?\" [1288] The girl answered immediately: \"I don't need to think, sir. I can assure you, I've never seen this man in my life!\" [1289] Ellery's calm and even voice broke the ensuing silence, and everyone turned to look at him in surprise. [1290] \"Miss Francis, forgive me for interrupting your speech,\" he said kindly, \"but I'm curious, did you notice how the man who approached you was dressed?\" [1291] Francis smiled at Ellery, and he winked back at her. \"I didn't pay much attention to his clothes, Mr. Queen,\" she said, flashing her bright white teeth, \"but I seem to remember him wearing a suit - with a stain on his shirtfront - like a wine stain - and a high-crowned hat. In my memory - of course, except for that stain on his shirt - he was very particular about his clothes and had good taste.\" [1292] Ellery quietly thanked her, then leaned back on the bookshelf. Mr. Queen looked sharply at his son and stood up. [1293] \"So, ladies and gentlemen, that's the end of the investigation. I think we can safely say that this matter is settled.\" [1294] There was a round of applause from the crowd, and everyone gathered around Francis, who was glowing with happiness. Barry, Pierce, and Eve Ellis escorted Francis triumphantly out of the room. Stanford looked sad and carefully extended his arm to his mother. [1295] \"The first lesson is over,\" he announced solemnly. \"Mother, hold on to my arm before you faint!\" Mrs. Avery-Pop swayed heavily on her son's arm, protesting as she left the room. [1296] Mr. Eves-Pope shook Queene's hand firmly. \"So, you think this matter is over with my daughter?\" he asked. [1297] \"Yes, Mr. Eves-Pope,\" the officer replied. \"Thank you for your hospitality, sir. We must be going now \u2013 there's much to do. Shall we go together, Henry?\" [1298] Five minutes later, Queene, Ellery, and District Attorney Sampson strode down Riverside Drive side by side, discussing the morning's events as they walked towards 72nd Street. [1299] \"I'm glad that line of inquiry has been cleared up and yielded nothing,\" Sampson mused. \"I must say, I'm impressed by the girl's courage, Que!\" [1300] \"A good kid,\" the officer said. \"What do you think, Ellery?\" he asked suddenly, turning to his son, who was staring straight ahead at the river. [1301] \"Oh, she's quite charming,\" Ellery replied quickly, his dazed eyes suddenly brightening. [1302] \"I'm not talking about the girl, my son,\" the father said impatiently. \"I'm talking about the overall situation this morning.\" [1303] \"Oh, that!\" Ellery smiled slightly. \"You don't mind if I quote an Aesop's fable, do you?\" [1304] \"I do mind,\" the father grumbled. [1305] \"The lion,\" Ellery said, \"may benefit from a mouse.\" [1306] Chapter 13: The Quinns on the Quinns\n[1307] At 6:30 that evening, after dinner, Juno had just cleared the table and was bringing coffee for the Quinns when the doorbell rang. The all-purpose helper straightened his tie, pulled down his jacket, and strode purposefully into the hallway (the police officer and Ellery watched him with interest). [1308] \"So formal, Juno!\" he muttered. \"Oh, oh! Dr. Plauti brings a guest. Let them in, kid!\" [1309] Juno went out again, bringing in the assistant medical examiner and a tall, thin man with a bald head and meticulously groomed beard. The Quinns stood up. [1310] \"I've been waiting to hear from you, doctor!\" Quinn grinned and shook Plauti's hand. \"If I'm not mistaken, this must be Professor Jones! Welcome to my home, doctor.\" The frail man bowed. [1311]\u201cThis is my son, my conscience keeper, doctor.\u201d Quinns added, introducing Ellery. \u201cEllery\u2014this is Dr. Siddles Jones.\u201d\n[1312]Dr. Jones extended his soft, large hand. \u201cSo, you must be the young man that Quinns and Sampson have been talking about!\u201d He spoke in a deep, resonant voice. \u201cIt's a pleasure to meet you, sir.\u201d\n[1313]\u201cI've been looking forward to meeting our city's Paracelsus, the famous toxicologist.\u201d Ellery smiled. \u201cYour name sends shivers down the spines of New Yorkers.\u201d He pretended to shudder and pointed to the chair next to him. The four sat down. [1314]\u201cHave some coffee, gentlemen,\u201d Quinns suggested, then called out to Juno, who was peeking out from the kitchen doorway. \u201cJuno! You little scamp! Bring us four cups of coffee!\u201d Juno grinned and disappeared behind the door. Soon he popped up, like a toy from a toy box, bearing four steaming cups of coffee. [1315]Plautti looked like the devil himself as he pulled a frightening-looking black cigar from his pocket and puffed away vigorously. [1316] \"Idle chatter is only suitable for people like you,\" he said after taking a puff of smoke, \"But me, I spend my days analyzing the contents of a woman's stomach, just so I can go home and get some sleep.\" [1317] \"I know, I know!\" Ellery muttered, \"I learned from seeking your help from Dr. Jones that you encountered difficulties in analyzing Mr. Field's body. Tell me, my Esquilp.\" [1318] \"I will,\" Plautus replied gravely. \"You're right - I encountered a major difficulty. Professionally speaking, I can humbly say that I have some experience in examining the organs of deceased ladies and gentlemen, but I admit I've never seen anyone's organs as messed up as this guy's. I mean it, Jones can vouch for that. For example, his esophagus and entire trachea looked like someone had sprayed them with a blowtorch.\" [1319] \"What - it couldn't be mercuric chloride, could it, doctor?\" Ellery asked. He was proud of his limited knowledge of precise science. [1320] \"No, impossible,\" Plautus said in a low voice. \"Let me tell you what happened. I analyzed all the poisons mentioned in the records, but despite its petroleum-like composition, I still couldn't pinpoint it accurately. Yes, sir - I'm truly at a loss. And let me tell you a secret - the coroner thought I was overworking myself and seeing things, so he tried it himself with that delicate Italian hand of his. The result, my friends, was zero. So, let's leave this problem to our fountain of knowledge and let him tell us his experience.\" [1321] Dr. Sauls Jones cleared his throat ominously. \"Thank you for the dramatic introduction, my friend,\" he said in a low voice. \"Yes, the body was turned over to me. At this point, I must say that this is the most shocking discovery in the fifteen years of my career at the toxicology office.\" [1322] \"Good heavens, heavens!\" Quinlow whispered, taking a pinch of snuff. \"I'm beginning to admire the intelligence of our murderer's friend. Recent events suggest that this murder is anything but ordinary. What did you find, doctor?\" [1323] \"I naturally assumed that Platt and the coroner had done a thorough job preparing,\" Dr. Jones began, crossing his bony legs, \"and they usually do. So, I first analyzed this little-known poison. Little-known, I mean, from the criminal's point of view. To illustrate how thorough my search was\u2014I even considered one of the backup poisons favored by our science fiction novelist friends: arrow poison, a toxin from South America. Five detective novels out of four use this poison. But even the most criticized arrow poison in its family disappointed me...\"\n[1324] Ellery leaned back and laughed. \"If you're being sarcastic about my profession, Dr. Jones, I can tell you that none of my novels have used arrow poison.\" [1325] The toxicologist's eyes lit up. \"So, you're a detective novelist too, eh? Quinlow, old pal, let me express my condolences to you... Nevertheless, gentlemen, when it comes to explaining the rarity of the poison, we can usually draw a clear conclusion\u2014namely, that it is a rare poison in the pharmacopoeia. Of course, we know very little about many rare poisons\u2014especially Eastern drugs.\" [1326] \"Well, to put it briefly, I found myself in a dilemma that made me very unhappy.\" Dr. Jones recalled the situation and chuckled softly, \"That conclusion was indeed unhappy. The poison I analyzed had some familiar properties, like Prouty said, but completely different other properties. I spent most of last night carefully studying my flasks and test tubes, and suddenly found the answer in the middle of the night.\" [1327] Ellery and Quin sat up straight, and Dr. Prouty sighed and relaxed in his chair, reaching for another cup of coffee. The toxicologist uncrossed his legs and spoke in a deep, resonant voice. [1328] \"The poison that killed your victim, officer, is called tetraethyl lead!\" [1329] For a scientist, Dr. Jones' high-pitched announcement would have had a dramatic effect. But for the detective, it meant nothing. For Ellery, he just murmured, \"Sounds like a mythical monster!\" [1330] Dr. Jones smiled slightly and continued, \"Didn't you notice that? Let me tell you about tetraethyl lead. It has almost no smell \u2013 more precisely, its physical properties are like trichloromethane, that's the first point. Second \u2013 it has a taste \u2013 of course, it's weak \u2013 but it can be confirmed that it smells like ether. Third \u2013 it is very toxic and terrifying. It is very toxic \u2013 let me tell you what kind of damage this potent chemical can do to living tissue.\" [1331] By now, the toxicologist had completely captured the attention of the audience. [1332] \"I took a healthy rabbit, the kind used for experiments, and painted undiluted tetraethyl lead on - notice, just a little - in the soft area behind the ear. Remember, this is not an internal injection, but only applied to the skin. It will be absorbed by the skin and then reach the blood. I observed the rabbit for an hour - after that, I didn't need to observe it anymore, it was dead, just like any other dead rabbit.\" [1333] \"I don't see it as very toxic, doctor,\" the officer questioned. [1334] \"Not strong? Well, believe me, it's extraordinary.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nIt was just a matter of painting a little on a whole piece of healthy skin - I'll tell you, I was shocked. If there were a wound on the skin or if it was ingested internally, that would be another matter. So, you can imagine what would happen to Field after swallowing this stuff - he drank a lot of it!\" [1335] Ellery's eyebrows furrowed in thought. He began to wipe the lenses of his spectacles. [1336] \"This is not all,\" Dr. Jones continued. \"As far as I know \u2013 I've been working in this city for years and I have some knowledge of developments in other parts of the world \u2013 but as far as I know, tetraethyl lead has never been used for criminal purposes.\" [1337] The detective sat up straight, his eyes widening. \"That's significant, doctor!\" he murmured. \"Are you sure?\" [1338] \"Of course. That's why I'm so interested.\" [1339] \"How long would it take to kill someone with this poison, doctor?\" Ellery asked slowly. [1340] Dr. Jones looked grim. \"I can't give you an exact answer because, as far as I know, no one has died from this poison before. But I can make a fairly accurate guess. I think Field would not have lasted more than fifteen minutes, maybe twenty, after ingesting this poison.\" [1341] Quin's cough broke the ensuing silence. \"On the other hand, doctor,\" he said, \"isn't the uniqueness of this poison what makes it easy to trace? What do you think is its most common source? Where does it come from? If I wanted to use it for criminal purposes without leaving a trace, how would I get it?\" [1342] The toxicologist's face twisted into a bitter smile. \"Tracking down this poison,\" he said earnestly, \"is up to you. You can look it up. What I can confirm right now - remember, it's almost completely new to us - tetraethyl lead is most commonly found in a certain type of petroleum product. I stumbled upon the simplest method to produce it in large quantities. You won't guess how. It can be extracted from the most common and ordinary gasoline.\" [1343] The Quin father and son exclaimed in a low voice, \"Gasoline!\" The detective shouted, \"Ah - but how are we supposed to trace it?\" [1344] \"The problem is here,\" the toxicologist replied. \"I can go to a gas station on the corner, fill up a tank, drive home, and extract tetraethyl lead from the gas tank with a little time and effort.\" [1345] \"Does that mean, doctor,\" Ellery interjected hopefully, \"that the killer needs some experience with chemical experiments - familiarity with chemical analysis, or something like that?\" [1346] \"It doesn't say that. Anyone with a home distillation apparatus can extract tetraethyl lead, and leave no trace. The advantage of the process is that the boiling point of tetraethyl lead is higher than any other component in gasoline. All you have to do is extract the other components at a certain temperature, and what's left is this poison.\" [1347] The officer took a pinch of snuff and his fingers trembled slightly. \"I can only say \u2013 I'm in awe of this killer,\" he muttered. \"Tell me, Doctor, does one need to have knowledge of toxicology to know this? If he doesn't have a particular interest in this area \u2013 and hasn't been trained in it \u2013 how could he know this?\" [1348] Dr. Jones snorted. \"Officer, you surprise me. Your question has been answered already.\" [1349] \"What's going on? What do you mean?\" [1350] \"I just told you how to extract it, didn't I? If you heard this from a toxicologist, and had a distillation room, wouldn't you be able to extract some too? You don't need any other knowledge except knowing the boiling point of tetraethyl lead. Give up, Quin! You have no chance of tracing the killer with this poison. It's possible he overheard two toxicologists, or even two doctors who had heard of this poison, mention it. The rest is easy. I'm not saying it's definitely so, the killer could be a chemist. But I'm just telling you all the possibilities.\" [1351] \"I suppose the poison was put in the whiskey, right, doctor?\" Quine asked, looking bewildered. [1352] \"That's certain,\" the toxicologist replied. \"There was a large amount of whiskey components in his stomach. Of course, it's easy to poison someone this way. Anyway, most whiskeys bought now have an ether flavor. Moreover, Field may have drunk it down without realizing it was tainted - if he could realize it at all.\" [1353] \"He wouldn't taste it, would he?\" Ellery asked wearily. [1354] \"I've never tasted it, young man, so I'm not sure,\" Dr. Jones replied somewhat sharply. \"But I doubt he could taste it - at least not enough to raise his suspicions. And once he drank it, whether he realized it or not made no difference.\" [1355] Quine turned to Plaut, who was now asleep and his cigar had gone out. \"Hey, doctor!\" [1356] Plaut opened his eyes sleepily. \"Where are my slippers - I can't seem to find them, damn it!\" [1357] Despite the tense atmosphere, the assistant coroner's words made everyone laugh. When he realized what he had said, he smiled and said, \"That's just proof that I should go home and sleep, Quine. What else do you want to know?\" [1358] \"Tell me,\" Quine said, still trembling slightly, \"what did you find out from the whiskey analysis?\" [1359] \"Oh!\" Plaut immediately woke up. \"The whiskey in the bottle is just like the other whiskeys I've tested - nothing wrong with it. These years, my main job has been to test the components of the alcohol. The smell of whiskey on Field's body made me think he had drunk some cheap stuff. But the Scottish whiskey and rye whiskey you brought me from his apartment are both very high quality. They may have been purchased from the same place, in fact. They could be imported. Since the war, I haven't seen such high-quality alcohol in the country - except for those stored before the war... I think Wiley has already given you my report, and the ginger ale is also fine.\" [1360] Quine nodded. \"Well, that seems to be settled,\" he said gravely. \"We seem to have made no progress on tetraethyl lead. But just to be on the safe side, doctor - work with the professors here to see if there are any flaws in the distribution of the poison in the body. You two are very familiar with it, and it will be more effective than other people. It's just a shot in the dark, and it may not yield any results.\" [1361] \"That's fine,\" Ellery whispered. \"Every novelist has to write until the end.\" [1362] * * *\n[1363] \"I think,\" after the two left, Ellery said urgently, \"I should go to the bookstore and buy Faulkner's novel.\" [1364] \"Sit here!\" the policeman shouted, pulling him back to the chair. \"No. That damn book won't run away. I want you to sit here, I have a headache, and you're not going anywhere.\" [1365] Ellery sat on the cushion, sighing. \"Whenever I think about how all the investigations into human weaknesses are useless and a waste of time, my respected father puts such a responsibility on me again. Hey! What do you think?\" [1366] \"I'm not putting any responsibility on you,\" Quine growled, \"Stop exaggerating, I'm already dizzy. I want you to help me straighten out this mess of a case and see - uh, if we can find anything.\" [1367] \"I'm skeptical,\" Ellery said, \"Where do I start?\" [1368] \"You don't need to say anything,\" dad grumbled, \"Tonight, I'll talk, you listen. You might want to take some notes. [1369] \"Let's start with Field. First, I think we can safely assume that our friend didn't go to the theater Monday night for entertainment, but for business. Agreed?\" [1370] \"In my opinion, that's a given,\" Ellery said, \"How did Willy report Field's activities on Monday?\" [1371] \"Field went to the office at 9:30\u2014he usually gets there in the morning. He worked until noon. There were no private visitors all morning. At 12:00, he had lunch alone at the Webster Club, returned to the office at 1:30, and worked until 4:00\u2014he seemed to go straight home after work, as the doorman and elevator operator can confirm he was back in his apartment at 4:30. Michaels went to him at 5:00 and left at 6:00, and Willy found no other information. Field left the apartment at 7:30, dressed as we found him. I have a list of clients he saw that day, but it's not very useful.\" [1372] \"What's the reason for his low bank account balance?\" Ellery asked. [1373] \"As I suspected,\" Quinn answered, \"Field has been losing a lot of money in the stock market\u2014not to mention he also wastes money at the racetrack. For a sensible man, he's a gullible fool. Anyway, that explains why his personal account cash is so low. Moreover, perhaps it can more firmly indicate that the '50,000' we found on the promotional material refers to money. And this money, I'm sure, is somehow related to the person he was supposed to meet at the theater.\" [1374] \"Now, I think we can safely conclude that Field is very familiar with the killer. On the one hand, he accepted the drink offered to him without the slightest suspicion, not even asking; on the other hand, they arranged to meet in a place that seemed to be for concealment\u2014otherwise, why choose the theater?\" [1375] \"Well, I'll ask you the same question,\" Ellery interrupted him, pursing his lips, \"Why choose the theater as the meeting place? It's obvious that they were going to engage in a secret, criminal transaction. Why not the park, which is more concealed? Or the hotel lobby, which is also good? How do you answer that?\" [1376] \"Unfortunately, my son,\" the detective said mildly, \"Field didn't suspect anyone was going to kill him. In his mind, he just had to do his part in the transaction. In fact, it's likely that Field chose the theater as the meeting place himself. Perhaps he wanted to create an alibi for himself. We can't know for sure what he was trying to do. As for the hotel lobby - he would have risked being seen by others. And perhaps he didn't want to go to a secluded place like the park. Finally, he may have had a specific reason not to be seen with the other person. Don't forget, the ticket stubs we found show that the other person didn't go into the theater with Field. But these are all pointless speculations -\"\n[1377] Ellery smiled thoughtfully but said nothing. He thought to himself that the old man hadn't fully answered his question, and for a detective like Quin, who was more direct in his thinking, this was unusual...\n[1378] But Quin continued speaking. \"Well, we must always remember that the person who Field was dealing with may not be the killer. Of course, this is just one possibility. This crime seems to have been well-planned. But if that person isn't the killer, we need to find two people in the audience on Monday night who have a direct connection to Field's death.\" [1379] \"Morgan?\" Ellery asked casually. [1380] The detective shrugged. \"Perhaps. Why didn't he tell us that when we spoke to him yesterday afternoon? He confessed to everything else. Well, maybe he felt that paying a blackmail fee to a murdered man and being at the theater was enough indirect evidence.\" [1381] \"Let's look at this,\" Ellery said. \"We found a number '5,000' written on the flyer by the deceased, which obviously refers to money. Through Sampson and Cronin, we know that Field was a despicable man and possibly a criminal. Moreover, we learned from Morgan that he was good at extortion. Therefore, I think we can safely assume that he went to the Roman Theater on Monday night to collect or arrange the payment of the $50,000 ransom from someone we don't know yet. So far, it all makes sense, doesn't it?\" [1382] \"Continue,\" the inspector grumbled without expressing his opinion. [1383] \"Okay,\" Ellery continued, \"if we assume that the person who was being extorted and the murderer are the same person, we don't need to look for a motive anymore. The motive is clear - to stop Field from continuing to extort. However, if we assume that the murderer and the person being extorted are two different people, we have to continue looking for his motive. My view is that there is no need to do so - the murderer and the person being extorted are the same. What do you think?\" [1384] \"I tend to agree with you, Ellery,\" the inspector said. \"I only mentioned another possibility - without reaching a conclusion. So, let's continue on the assumption that the murderer and the person being extorted are the same...\"\n[1385] \"Now - I want to find out about the missing tickets.\" [1386] \"Ah \u2013 missing tickets,\" Ellery murmured. \"I wonder what you can make of that?\" [1387] \"Be serious now, you little rascal!\" Quin shouted. \"That's all I know. Overall, we have to deal with eight seats \u2013 one of which belongs to Field, and we found the stub on him; one belongs to the killer, and Flint found the stub for us; and finally, there are six empty seats, with tickets sold according to the ticket office records, but nowhere in the theater or the ticket office were the stubs found, either torn or intact \u2013 nothing at all. First, it's highly unlikely that all six tickets were in the theater on Monday evening, and then someone took them out. Remember, the search of everyone that night wasn't very thorough, and it's possible that we overlooked something small like a ticket. However, that's basically impossible. The best explanation is that either Field or the killer bought eight tickets, intending to use two and leave the other six, ensuring that they could not be disturbed during their brief transaction. In this case, the wisest course of action would be to destroy the tickets as soon as they were purchased. It could have been done by either Field or the killer, depending on who arranged the meeting. So, we must forget about those six tickets \u2013 they no longer exist, and we'll never find them. [1388] \"Next,\" the detective continued, \"we learned that Field and the person he was extorting entered the theater separately. This can be inferred from the fact that when I put the two stubs together, the tear didn't line up. If two people entered the theater together, the tickets would be handed to the usher together, and they would be torn together. This doesn't mean they didn't enter the theater at the same time; they may have come in one after the other for safety, pretending not to know each other. However, Maggie O'Connell claimed that no one sat in LL30 during the first act, and Jesse Lynch, the boy selling oranges, confirmed that no one sat there for at least ten minutes after the second act began. That means either the killer hadn't entered the theater yet or he had come in but sat somewhere else \u2013 he bought another ticket. \" [1389] Ellery shook his head. [1390] \"I realized that too, son,\" the old man said, a little irritated. \"I was just working through the thought process. What I meant to say was that it seems highly unlikely that the killer entered the theater at the time of the performance; he probably came in after the second act started and at least ten minutes later.\" [1391] \"I can prove that,\" Ellery said lazily. [1392] The officer took a pinch of snuff. \"I know - the mysterious numbers on the flyers. What do you make of them?\" [1393] 930\n[1394] 815\n[1395] 50,000\n[1396] \"We know what 50,000 represents. The other two numbers, however, do not refer to money but to time. First, '815' \u2013 the play started at 8:25. It is likely that Field arrived around 8:15 or even earlier, as he glanced at his watch for some reason. Now, let's assume that he had an appointment with someone, but that person was late. It is very likely that Field jotted down the numbers on the flyer casually \u2013 first, '50,000', indicating the transaction he was considering, including the $50,000 ransom; then 8:15, the time he was considering the matter; and finally 9:30 \u2013 the time the victim should have arrived! Writing these things down was natural for Field, as anyone with a habit of scribbling in their spare time would do so. We are lucky, as this indicates two things: first, the exact time Field met with the killer was 9:30; second, it corresponds to the time we hypothesize the murder took place. At 9:25, Lynch saw Field alive and alone; by 9:30, according to what Field wrote, the killer should have arrived, and we naturally assume the killer did arrive at the theater; and according to Dr. Jones, the poison that killed Field took 15 to 20 minutes to take effect \u2013 considering that Paskack found the body at 9:55, we can infer that the poison was taken at 9:35. If the tetrachloroethylene took 20 minutes to take effect \u2013 that would be 9:55. Of course, the killer had already left the crime scene by then. Don't forget, he couldn't have known that Mr. Paskack would suddenly stand up and leave his seat. The killer may have thought that Field's body would not be discovered until intermission, at 10:05, so Field would have been dead and unable to leave any further information. The killer was undoubtedly lucky, as he discovered Field too late and did not leave any other information, only stating that he was murdered. If Paskack had left five minutes earlier, we would have caught the cunning killer by now.\" [1397] \"Great!\" Ellery murmured, his smile full of affection, \"That was well stated, congratulations!\" [1398] \"Oh, go to hell,\" his father barked, \"I was just repeating what you said Monday night in Penczel's office \u2013 in fact, although the killer left the crime scene between 9:30 and 9:55, he was still in the theater for the rest of the evening, until we got everyone to leave.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 3:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nI'm here as quickly as I can. Where's the victim?\" [263] Detective Quin pointed to the corpse on the ground, and Plautti immediately knelt by the corpse on the pavement, starting to work. A police officer was called over to provide a flashlight for the assistant medical examiner. [264] Detective Quin pulled Jesse's arm to the side and asked, \"Jesse, what happened after he asked you to buy ginger ale?\" [265] The boy watched the proceedings, took a deep breath when the officer asked, and continued, \"Well, sir, I told him we don't sell ginger ale, only orange water. He leaned forward slightly, and I could smell the alcohol on his breath. Then, he whispered to me, 'If you go and get me a bottle, I'll give you fifty cents, kid. But I need it right away.' You know, no one gives tips anymore...well, I told him I couldn't go right away, but I could slip out and get it for him when the second act started. He agreed, gave me his seat number, and walked away. I watched him go back into the theater. After the intermission ended, the usher closed the door, and I left the alleyway stall, crossed the street, and ran to Libby's ice cream shop. I -\"\n[266] \"Jesse, do you often leave your stall?\" [267] \"No, sir. I usually bring everything with me and run inside the theater before the usher closes the door, then move my stall to the downstairs lounge. But the man said he needed the ginger ale right away, and I thought I could get it for him first, which would be quicker. Then I'd come back to the aisle, grab my stuff, and go in through the front door. Nobody would say anything...well, I left my stall, ran to Lippy's, bought a bottle of ginger ale, and sneaked back into the theater to find the man. He gave me a dollar. I thought he was a nice guy, because he said he'd only give me fifty cents.\" [268] \"Jesse, you've done well,\" Inspector Queen said approvingly. \"Now, just a few more questions. Was he sitting in this seat? Did he ask you to come here?\" [269] \"Yes, sir. He said it was LL32 left, and I found him right here.\" [270] \"Good.\u201d Inspector Queen paused for a moment before asking casually, \"Jesse, did you notice if he was alone?\" [271] \"Yes, sir. He was alone. I noticed that because the theater is usually full since it opened, and I found it strange that he had so many empty seats around him.\" [272] \"Jesse, you did well. You would make a good detective... I think you don't remember how many empty seats there were?\" [273] \"Well, sir, it was quite dark in the theater, and I didn't pay much attention. I think there were probably six in total, a few in his row, next to him, and a few in the front, right in front of him.\" [274] \"Jesse, wait a moment.\" [275] The boy turned around, licking his lips, and was clearly a bit afraid when he heard Ellery's low, cold voice. [276] \"Did you see his fancy hat again when you served him the ginger ale, Jesse?\" Ellery asked, tapping the tip of his cane on his clean shoe. [277] \"Ah, yes... yes, sir.\" The boy stammered, \"His hat was on his knees when I served him the ginger ale, but before I left, I saw him put it under the seat.\" [278] \"One more question, Jesse.\" The boy breathed a sigh of relief at the sound of the officer's encouraging voice. \"Do you think it took you about ten minutes to get the drink to him after the second act started?\" [279] Jesse Lynch thought carefully before answering confidently, \"About ten minutes, sir. We had to keep track of time. I said ten minutes because when I came in with the bottle, the stage was at the part where the girl was captured in the gang's hideout and being interrogated by the thugs.\" [280] \"You're a sharp-eyed Hermes, young man!\" Ellery exclaimed softly, suddenly smiling. Jesse's last trace of fear disappeared as he saw the smile. He smiled back at Ellery. Ellery leaned in and asked, \"Now, tell me, boy, how could it have taken you ten minutes to buy a bottle of ginger ale, walk back to the theater, and return? That's quite a long time, isn't it?\" [281] Jesse's face turned red, and he looked from Ellery to Quin, blushing. \"Uh, sir... I think it's because I stopped for a while and chatted with my girlfriend.\" [282] \"Your girlfriend?\" Quin asked curiously. [283] \"Yes, sir. Her name is Eleanor Lively, and her father owns the ice cream shop. When I went to buy the ginger beer, she... she wanted me to stay and hang out with her.\" [284] \"The ice cream shop's alleyway stall?\" Ellery said eagerly. \"Right, Jesse, your stall is in the alleyway. You're not telling me you went back to the alleyway just because you felt like it.\" [285] \"Of course we went back!\" the boy answered, surprised by Ellery's reaction. \"I mean, both of us went back, me and Eleanor.\" [286] \"You and Eleanor! Ah, Jesse,\" Ellery said calmly. \"How long were you there?\" [287] Detective Queene's eyes gleamed with interest as he murmured approvingly and listened attentively to the boy's answer. [288] \"Well, I wanted to move my booth away as soon as possible, sir. But Eleanor and I... we wanted to chat, so Eleanor suggested that we could stay in the alley until the next intermission. I thought it was a good idea, so I decided to wait until the act was almost over, around ten minutes to ten, and then sneak in to get some orange juice. When the usher opened the door for the second intermission, I'd be ready. So, we left the booth there, sir... that's not wrong, is it, sir? I didn't want to do anything bad.\" [289] Ellery sat up straight, looking directly into the boy's eyes. \"Jesse, I hope you'll answer me carefully: What exact time did you and Eleanor go back to the alley?\" [290] \"Uh...,\" Jesse scratched his head. \"I gave the ginger ale to that person around nine twenty-five. Then I went to find Eleanor, stayed with her for a while, and then went back to the alley. I got back to my booth... uh... probably around nine thirty-five.\" [291] \"Good. What time did you leave the alley exactly?\" [292] \"Ten o'clock, sir. I asked Eleanor if I should go get the orange juice, and she looked at her watch, and it was ten o'clock.\" [293] \"Did you hear anything about what happened at the theater?\" [294] \"No, sir. I think we were just chatting... I had no idea anything happened at the theater. Later, I left the alley and ran into Johnny Tice, the usher, who was standing there like a guard. He told me that something happened at the theater, and Mr. Pensel ordered him to stand outside the left alley. That's when I found out.\" [295] \"I see...,\" Ellery said, somewhat excitedly as he took off his glasses and waved them in front of the boy's nose, \"Jesse, think carefully now, was anyone going in or out of the alley while you and Eleanor were there?\" [296] The boy answered promptly and confidently, \"No, sir, not a single person.\" [297] \"Alright, young man.\" Detective Quin gently patted the boy's back, and the boy smiled and left at Quin's prompting. Quin looked around sharply and observed Mr. Pencroft closely, noticing that his announcement on stage didn't seem to have any effect. So, Quin beckoned him over and said, \"Mr. Pencroft, I'd like to know the schedule of the play...what time does Act Two start?\" [298] \"Act Two starts officially at 9:15 and ends at 10:05.\" Mr. Pencroft answered promptly. [299] \"So, was the performance tonight scheduled according to that?\" [300]\n[301] \"Of course,\" the manager said. \"Due to cues, lighting, and other factors, we must be on time.\" [302] Officer Quinn calculated silently in his mind, \"So, at 9:25, the boy saw Field alive.\" [303] Officer Quinn suddenly turned around and called out to Detective Dahl. Detective Dahl ran over. [304] \"Detective Dahl,\" the officer asked, \"Do you remember when Pazak told you the exact time he found the murder?\" [305] Detective Dahl scratched his head. \"I can't remember, sir,\" he said. \"I just remember that it was almost the end of Act Two when it happened.\" [306] \"That's too vague, Detective Dohr,\" said the officer impatiently. \"Where are the actors?\" [307] \"We have them gathered over there, just behind the foyer, sir,\" answered Detective Dohr. \"We don't know what else to do with them.\" [308] \"Bring one over,\" the officer ordered sharply. [309] Detective Dohr ran off. Quien Officer beckoned to Piggott, who was only a few feet away, standing between a man and a woman. [310] \"Bring the doorman over, Piggott,\" ordered Quien Officer. Piggott nodded and brought over a waddling, overweight tall man in a uniform whose clothes clung tightly to his sagging muscles, and whose hat trembled in his hand. [311] \"Are you the doorman standing outside the theater?\" the officer asked. [312] \"Yes, sir,\" the doorman answered, nervously fidgeting with his hat. [313] \"Good. Now, think carefully, has anyone - I mean anyone - left the theater during the second act?\" [314] The old man thought for a moment and answered firmly, \"No, sir, no one has left the theater. I mean, except for the boy selling orange juice, no one has left.\" [315] \"Were you there the whole time?\" Quine officer asked loudly. [316] \"Yes, sir.\" [317] \"So, do you remember anyone going in during the second act?\" [318] \"Well, uh... Jesse Lynch, the boy selling orange juice, went in at the beginning of the second act.\" [319] \"What about the others?\" [320] The old man did not speak, but instead tried to think hard. After a while, he looked from one face to another, his eyes filled with despair. Then, he murmured, \"I don't remember, sir.\" [321] Inspector Quin looked at him impatiently. The old man seemed tense and sincere. He was sweating profusely and occasionally glanced at Pendergast, as if he suspected that his poor memory might cost him his job. [322] \"I'm really sorry, sir,\" the porter said again. \"Really sorry. It seems someone did come in, but my memory isn't what it used to be. I... I can't seem to remember.\" [323] Ellery interrupted the old man's heavy-accented speech with a calm tone. [324] \"How long have you been the doorkeeper?\" [325] The old man turned his confused gaze to the new interrogator. \"About ten years, sir. I wasn't a doorkeeper before, but as I got older, I couldn't do much else...\"\n[326] \"I understand,\" Ellery said gently. He hesitated for a moment before firmly saying, \"As a doorkeeper for so many years, you might have forgotten if someone came in during the first act. But usually, very few people come in during the second act. So, no matter what, you should be able to remember if you think hard about it.\" [327] The old man replied with a pained expression, \"I... I really can't remember, sir. I could say no one came in, but that wouldn't be true. But I just can't answer.\" [328] \"It's okay,\" Detective Quinn said, placing his hand on the old man's shoulder. \"Never mind, we may have asked too much. Let's stop here for now.\" The poor old man shuffled away. [329] At this point, Dr. Dohle walked towards the crowd, followed by a tall, handsome man wearing a tweed coat and still carrying traces of stage makeup on his face. [330] \"This is Mr. Peel, the lead actor in the play,\" Dr. Dohle reported. [331] Detective Quean smiled at the actor and extended his hand. \"Nice to meet you, Mr. Peel. Perhaps you can provide me with a clue.\" [332] \"It's my pleasure to assist you, officer,\" Peel replied in a deep voice. He glanced at the back of the pathologist busy with the body and then looked away with a look of disgust. [333] \"I suppose you were on stage when the audience started shouting, right?\" Detective Quean asked. [334] \"Yes, in fact, everyone from the cast was there. What do you want to know?\" [335] \"Can you tell me the exact time when you noticed the commotion in the audience?\" [336] \"Sure. There were about ten minutes left in the show. It was the climax of the play, and my character was supposed to shoot. We had discussed this point during rehearsals, so we were pretty sure of the time.\" [337] Quinn nodded. \"Thank you, Mr. Pierce, that's exactly what I wanted to know... by the way, I apologize for having you all crammed back here. We've been very busy and couldn't make any other arrangements, so please bear with us. You and the rest of the cast can now go backstage. Of course, please don't leave until you're notified.\" [338] \"I understand, officer. It's nice to be able to help,\" Pierce bowed and returned to the back of the theater. [339] Quinn leaned against the nearest chair and fell into thought. Ellery stood beside him, absent-mindedly wiping his glasses. [340] \"Well, Ellery?\" Quinn asked quietly. [341] \"Just preliminary information, my dear Watson.\" Watson mutters, \"Someone saw our 'esteemed' victim alive at 9:25, and then found him dead at 9:55. The question is: what happened in between? It sounds simple enough, but...\"\n[342] \"Not necessarily.\" Detective Quin grumbles, \"Pigott!\" [343] \"Yes, sir.\" [344] \"This is the usher, isn't it? Have her come over here.\" [345] Pigott releases the arm of the young woman standing beside him. She is elegantly dressed, heavily made up, with a dazzling smile that seems almost sinister. She saunters over, brazenly inspecting Detective Quin. [346] \"Are you the fixed usher in this aisle, Miss? What's your name?\" Detective Quinn asked in a casual tone. [347] \"Yes, I am. My name is O'Connell, Maggie O'Connell.\" [348] Detective Quinn gently took her arm, saying, \"I'm afraid you'll have to be brave, dear. Come over here for a moment.\" When they stopped at LL row, the girl's face turned pale. \"Sorry to interrupt, Dr. Plautti, hope you don't mind.\" Detective Quinn said. [349] Dr. Plautti looked up, frowning. \"Not at all, officer. I'll be done shortly.\" He stood up, holding a cigar, and moved to the side. [350] The girl leaned over the body, and Detective Quinn closely observed her expression. She took a deep breath. [351] \"Do you remember bringing this person to his seat, Miss O'Connell?\" [352] The girl hesitated, then said, \"I think I remember this person, but, as usual, I've been very busy tonight, giving seats to almost two hundred people, so I'm not entirely sure.\" [353] \"Do you remember these empty seats \u2014\" Inspector Quinn pointed to the seven empty seats, \"\u2014 were empty during the first act?\" [354] \"Uh... it seemed like this way when I walked up and down the aisle... yes, no one was sitting here. I remember no one sitting there all night.\" [355] \"Did anyone walk up and down the aisle during the second act, Miss O'Connell? Try to remember, your answer is crucial.\" [356] The girl hesitated again, casting a quick glance at the stern officer before saying, \"No... I didn't see anyone walking up and down the aisle. I can't give you much to go on, I know nothing about this. I'm just a hardworking girl, I -\"\n[357] \"Yes, yes, dear, we understand. Where do you usually stand when you're not seating people?\" [358] The girl pointed to the front of the aisle. [359] \"Were you in that spot during the second act, Miss O'Connell?\" Sergeant Queen asked with a smile. [360] The girl licked her lips and said, \"Well, yes, I was. But honestly, I didn't notice anything out of the ordinary all night.\" [361] \"Very well,\" the detective said in a calm tone, \"that's all for now.\" The girl turned and walked away briskly. [362] There was a small commotion in the crowd. The detective approached Plautti, who stood up, closing his box with a somewhat gloomy whistle. [363] \"Well, doctor, I assume you're done with the examination. What's the verdict?\" the detective asked. [364] \"The conclusion is quite simple, sir. He died two hours ago. At first, I was puzzled by the cause of death, but now I'm sure it was poison. The signs indicate that he died from some kind of alcohol poisoning, which you may have noticed from his yellowish skin. And you probably smelled the alcohol in his mouth, which was the most fragrant I've ever smelled. He was certainly drunk out of his mind. However, this was not just a regular case of alcohol poisoning, otherwise he wouldn't have died so quickly. That's all I can tell you for now.\" [365] The detective took out a bottle wrapped in a handkerchief from his pocket and handed it to Dr. Plautti, saying, \"This is the deceased's, doctor. I hope you can help me analyze the contents. Before that, let Jimmy take it to the lab for a detailed check for fingerprints. Oh, and - wait a moment.\" The detective looked around, picked up the half-empty bottle of ginger ale that had fallen on the carpet corner, \"Please analyze this ginger ale for me, doctor.\" He added. [366] The assistant coroner put the bottle of liquor and the bottle of ginger ale in his bag and adjusted his hat lightly. [367] \"Well, sir, I'm leaving. I'll give you a more detailed report after the autopsy, which should help with your investigation.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHe was sent to Elmira on a 'minor theft' charge thanks to Field's clever manipulation of the law. However, Field possessed evidence of the true crime Mickles had committed and hid it in his favorite collection, intending to use it at a later date. This Field, what a cunning man... After Mickles was released, Field used these documents to coerce Mickles into doing various tasks for him without any remorse. [2192] \"For a long time, Michael has been searching for those files. His eagerness, you can imagine. He would go to the apartment whenever he had the chance. After one failure after another, he became desperate. I have no doubt that when Field found out that Michael was rummaging through his apartment day after day, he must have been laughing behind his back... On Monday night, Michael went home to sleep as he said. But on Tuesday morning, when he read the newspaper and learned that Field had been murdered, he realized it was all over. He had to search desperately for those files - if he couldn't find them, the police might find them, and then he would be finished. So, on Tuesday morning, he risked being caught by the police and returned to Field's house. Of course, the whole story about the check was just nonsense. [2193] \"But let's go back to Barry. We found a file labeled 'Miscellaneous' in the hat, detailing an unsavory matter. To abbreviate, Stephen Barry has black blood in his veins. Born into a poor family in the South, there is concrete evidence - letters, birth certificates, and so on - proving his black heritage. As we know, Field has made a business out of pursuing such things. He somehow acquired these documents, how long ago we do not know, but it must have been a long time. He had then discovered Barry's identity, finding him a struggling actor living in poverty and penniless. Field decided to leave him be for the time being. Once Barry made it big or became famous, there would be plenty of time to extort him. What Field did not anticipate was Barry's engagement to the daughter of a millionaire, the aristocratic socialite Frances Evans-Pope. It goes without saying that if Frances Evans-Pope were to find out about Barry's mixed heritage, the consequences would be dire. Furthermore, it is crucial to note that Barry had become impoverished due to gambling. His earnings had gone into the pockets of the racecourse bookmaker's agent, and he owed a significant amount of money, which he would never be able to repay with his current means, unless he married Frances. In fact, he was so destitute that he secretly urged her to marry him as soon as possible. I have always been curious about how sincerely he treated Frances. However, to be fair, I do not believe he married her solely for the money. I think he genuinely loved her - but who doesn't? But who doesn't?\" [2194] The old man smiled as he reminisced, continuing, \"Not long ago, Field brought those documents to Barry \u2013 of course, secretly. Barry gave him all the money he could, but it was barely enough to satisfy the insatiable extortionist. With no other option, he could only keep delaying Field. But Field himself was in a bind due to gambling, trying to 'recoup' funds through one small deal after another. Barry was at a dead end, realizing that unless he made Field forever silent, he would lose everything. So he planned this murder. He understood that even if he somehow managed to gather the $50,000 Field demanded \u2013 clearly an impossible task \u2013 even if he got the original documents, Field would still ruin his hopes by spreading the word. The only way out was \u2013 eliminate Field. And he did.\" [2195] \"Black blood, huh?\" Crooning grumbled, \"Poor fellow.\" [2196] \"You couldn't tell from his looks,\" said Sampson, \"he looked just as white as you and me.\" [2197] \"Barry is not like a pure black man,\" the officer countered, \"He has one drop of black blood in his veins \u2013 just one drop, but that's enough for the Evans-Pope family... Continue on. After reading these documents, we understood everything: who did it, how it was done, and the motive. But we have to make a judgment on the evidence to convict. You can't accuse someone of murder without evidence... er, what do we have? Nothing! [2198] \"Let's discuss some useful clues that could serve as evidence. The handbag of the woman - eliminate. It's worthless, you know. The source of the poison - can't find it. By the way, Barry did it according to Dr. Jones' method - the toxicologist Jones. Barry bought regular gasoline, distilled it to get tetraethyl lead. No trace left. Another possible clue - Monte Field's hat, but it's gone... Six empty tickets - we've never seen them, and the chances of seeing them are slim. The only physical evidence - those documents - only explain the motive, but can't prove anything. With this evidence, any member of Morgan or Field's gang could have committed the crime.\" [2199] \"The only hope of securing incriminating evidence lies in sending someone to sneak into Barry's apartment at night, hoping to find the hat, tickets, or other clues, such as poison or poisonous instruments. I have engaged a specialist in burglary for this purpose, who will break into Barry's apartment on Friday night while he is at the theater performing, but no such clues have been found. The hat, tickets, and poison have all been destroyed. It is clear that Barry did it, we can be sure.\" [2200] \"I gathered a group of Monday night viewers in desperation and held a meeting in the hope that someone would remember seeing Barry that night. You know, sometimes people forget things due to tension or excitement, but then they remember later. But this time it didn't work either. The only valuable testimony was from the boy selling orange juice, who saw Field pick up a handbag used at the evening party in the alley. However, this had nothing to do with Barry. Remember, on Thursday night, when we questioned the cast members, we didn't get any direct evidence either. [2201] \"So we have a perfect, hypothetical statement of fact to present to the jury, with no concrete evidence. A clever defense lawyer could easily refute our case. It's mostly indirect evidence based on reasoning. You and I both know how much of a chance we have in court... Then, my real trouble came, and Ellery was leaving town. [2202] \"I racked my brain - I was left with little. How can I convict someone without evidence? It's so frustrating. Ellery sent me a telegram with a suggestion that helped me one last time.\" [2203] \"A suggestion?\" asked Cronin. [2204] \"He suggested that I personally engage in extortion...\"\n[2205] \"You personally extort?\" Sampson looked at him. \"I don't understand.\" [2206] \"Trust Ellery, he's good at solving puzzles.\" The police officer refutes, \"I immediately understand that the only thing I can do is fabricate evidence!\" [2207] Sampson and Cronin frown in confusion. [2208] \"It's simple,\" Quine says, \"Field was killed by an unusual poison. The reason Field was killed was because he blackmailed Barry. If Barry was blackmailed in the same situation, he would use poison again - probably the same poison. My hypothesis is reasonable, right? I don't need to say that you both know, 'once bitten, twice shy.' As for Barry, if I can lure him into using tetraethyl lead on someone else, I can catch him! This poison is almost unknown - but I don't need to explain further. You can see that if I catch him with tetraethyl lead on him, that's all the evidence I need. [2209] \"How to accomplish this feat is another matter... The situation was perfect for blackmailing. In fact, I have the original documents about Barry's background and impure blood. Barry thought these documents had been destroyed - he had no reason to suspect that the ones he got from Field were carefully forged. If I blackmail him, then his situation will be the same again. So he must resort to the same tactics. [2210] \"So I took advantage of our friend Charles Michaels. I used Michaels only because, in Barry's eyes, Michaels was Field's friend, henchman, and loyal partner, and it was reasonable for him to possess the original documents. I had Michaels write a letter, dictated by me. I had Michaels write it because Barry had dealt with Field and might recognize Michaels' handwriting. This seemed like a small problem, but I couldn't take any risks. If this plan went wrong, Barry would immediately recognize it, and I would never catch him.\" [2211] \"In the letter, I attached an original document to show that this new blackmail threat was potent. I claimed that Field had given Barry a copy - the attached document proved my point. Barry didn't doubt that Michaels was imitating his master and blackmailing him. The wording was like a final ultimatum. I set a time and place, and in short, the scheme worked...\n[2212] \"I think that's all, gentlemen. Barry came, with his faithful little syringe subcutaneously injected with tetraethyl lead, and a bottle of wine - you see, except for the location, it was exactly the same as the Field case. I instructed my man - Ritter - not to take a risk. As soon as he recognized Barry, he aimed his gun at him and sounded the alarm. Fortunately, we were almost right behind them in the bushes. Barry was desperate, and if he had had even the slightest chance, he would have killed Ritter and committed suicide.\" [2213] The officer finished speaking and sighed, leaning forward to snort some snuff, and the room fell silent. [2214] Sampson shifted in his chair. \"It sounds like a thriller, Quin,\" he said admiringly, \"but there are a few things I don't understand. For example, since tetraethyl lead is unknown to people, how did Barry discover it - to the point where he could refine it himself?\" [2215] \"Oh,\" the officer smiled, \"Ever since Jones described this poison, this has bothered me. Even after catching Barry, I was still in the dark. But this shows how stupid I am - the answer has been right in front of my nose. Do you remember, when we were at the Eves-Pope estate, a doctor named Cornish was introduced? This doctor is a private friend of the old financier, and both of them are interested in medicine. In fact, I remember Ellery asking, 'Did Eves-Pope recently donate $100,000 to the Chemical Research Foundation?' Indeed, it was. One evening a few months ago, a meeting was held at the Eves-Pope house, and Barry happened to hear about tetraethyl lead. Through Cornish's introduction, a group of scientists visited this financial giant to request funding for the foundation. The evening's conversation naturally turned to rumors in medicine and recent scientific discoveries. Barry admitted that he had overheard the council member, a famous toxicologist, introducing this poison to the members. At the time, Barry didn't think he would ever use this knowledge; when he decided to kill Field, he saw the superiority of this poison and its difficult traceability.\" [2216] \"What was the significance of the message you sent me through Louis Ponzel on Thursday night, Inspector?\" Cromwell asked curiously, \"Do you remember? You asked me to observe whether Ponzel and Ponzel knew each other when they met at Louin? As I reported to you, I asked Louin, and he denied knowing Ponzel. What's going on?\" [2217] \"Ponzel,\" the policeman whispered, \"Ponzel always piques my curiosity, Tim. When I sent him to you, remember, I hadn't made any deductions about the hat... I sent him to you out of curiosity. I thought that if Louin recognized him, it might suggest a connection between Ponzel and Field. My idea wasn't confirmed, and I didn't have high hopes to begin with. Ponzel might have known Field outside, while Louin didn't know. On the other hand, I didn't want Ponzel to hang around the theater that morning; it was beneficial for both of us for him to run errands.\" [2218] \"Oh, I hope you're satisfied with the package of newspapers I sent you according to your request,\" Cromwell smiled. [2219] \"What about the anonymous letter that Morgan received? Was it a cover-up or something else?\" Sampson asked. [2220] \"It was a deceptive trick,\" Quayne said solemnly. \"Barry explained it to me last night. He heard that Morgan threatened to kill Field. Of course, he didn't know that Field was blackmailing Morgan. But he thought that if he could trick Morgan into coming to the theater on Monday night, it would create a strong false lead. If Morgan didn't come, there would be no loss. That's how he operated: he chose ordinary cheap paper, put on gloves, and printed the letter at a print shop in a messy way, signed it with a meaningless initial, and then mailed it from the post office. He was careful with his fingerprints, so he couldn't be traced from the letter. Coincidentally, Morgan fell for it and came\n[2221] Sampson nodded. \"I have one more question. How did Barry arrange for the tickets? Was it all his doing?\" [2222] \"Of course, it was. Barry convinced Field that, for fairness, the meeting and the document exchange should take place secretly in the theater. Field agreed and was easily convinced to buy eight tickets at the ticket office. He realized that, for secrecy, he needed to buy six additional tickets. He gave Barry seven and kept one for himself, which he destroyed immediately.\" [2223] The officer stood up, smiling tiredly. \"Juna! Add some coffee,\" he whispered. [2224] Sampson waved Juna away. \"Thank you, Quin, I have to go. Cromwell and I have a lot of things to deal with regarding this gang. But I can't leave until I hear the whole story from you... Quin, old friend, I truly mean it, you did an incredible job.\" [2225] \"I've never heard of such a thing,\" Cromwell said sincerely. \"A puzzle to solve, and such a perfect reasoning from start to finish!\" [2226] \"Really?\" the officer asked calmly. \"I'm glad, gentlemen. All the credit goes to Ellery. I'm so proud of my son...\"\n[2227] Sampson and Cronin left, and Juna went back to the kitchen to wash the breakfast dishes. The officer turned to the desk, picked up a pen, and quickly read the letter he had written to his son. He sighed and continued writing. [2228] Forget what I just wrote. It's been over an hour since then. Sampson and Cronin came by, and I told them all about the work we did on the case. I've never seen such a pair! They were like children, listening to a fairy tale... As I said, I know how little I've done, and how much you've done. I'm looking forward to you finding a good girl to marry, and then the Quin family will settle down and live peacefully in Italy...\n[2229] When are you coming home? Don't think I'm rushing you, but I'm so lonely, son. I... no, I mean, I'm selfish and tired. An old relic like me needs some care. You'll be home soon, won't you? Juna sends his greetings. That mischievous fellow is washing dishes in the kitchen, making my ears ring. [2230] Love, Dad\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nPusak stood up to squeeze past Field, and at that moment, Field was already poisoned. Pusak heard him murmur, \"Murder! Murdered!\" or something similar. [708] \"Pusak called a policeman, who then brought a doctor from the audience to check if Field was dead. The doctor confirmed that the victim had died from some kind of alcohol poisoning. Later, Assistant Coroner Plauti confirmed this, but he also added that there was an interference factor - usually, even if someone dies from alcohol poisoning, it won't happen so quickly. So, we can put aside the specific cause of death for now, as it can only be determined after autopsy. [709] \"Since there were many people in the audience and order needed to be maintained, the policeman called for help. The nearby police arrived to assist. They immediately launched an investigation. The first important question was whether the killer had the opportunity to leave the crime scene after the murder. Detective Doyle, who was the first to arrive at the crime scene, immediately ordered the theater manager to place people at every exit and both sides of the corridors to guard them. [710] \"When I arrived here, I immediately thought of this point and conducted a simple investigation myself. I circled all the exits and asked the people guarding them. I found that there was someone guarding every door in the theater during the first and second acts, of course, there were two exceptions that I will mention later. Now, through the boy who sells orange juice, Jesse Lynch, we can confirm that the victim was still alive not only during the intermission between the first and second acts - Lynch had seen him in the corridor and talked to him - but also about ten minutes after the second act began, when Lynch had delivered ginger ale to his seat, where he was later found dead. There is a usher standing under the stairs leading to the second floor in the theater, who swears that no one went up or down during the second act. This also rules out the possibility of the killer going upstairs. [711] \"The two exceptions I mentioned are the two doors at the leftmost aisle, which should have been guarded but were not, because the usher Maggie O'Connell was sitting next to her sweetheart at the time. This made me think of a possibility that the killer might have left through one of these two doors. If the killer was so talented, these two doors would be very convenient for escape. But the usher's testimony ruled out this possibility. This was something I discovered after my father asked her, and I followed up with more questions. [712] \"You snuck up on her, you little brat?\" Quinns glared at Ellery. \"I did talk to her,\" Ellery said with a smile. \"And I found an important fact that is closely related to our current investigation. O'Connell swore that she locked the door inside and out with the floor lock before leaving the two doors and sitting next to Johnny Parsen. When the commotion started, she immediately stood up from the 'priest' and ran to the door, only to find it still locked, just like when she left. So, she opened the door, and at that moment, Detective Dohal was trying to get the audience to quiet down. Unless she lied - but I don't think so. This also proves that the killer did not leave through these two doors, as the door was still locked from the inside when the body was discovered.\" [713] \"Well, I guess I'll have to change careers!\" Quinns grumbled. \"She didn't tell me anything about this, you damn woman! I'll deal with her later, this hateful woman!\" [714] \"Please be rational, Mr. Peacekeeper,\" Ellery said with a smile. \"She didn't tell you about locking the door because you didn't ask her. And she probably felt bad enough already.\" [715] \"The floor lock was a critical piece of evidence that helped us to narrow down the list of suspects to the two ushers, Maggie O'Connell and Jesse Lynch. However, the fact that the\n[716] \"Anyway, through her story, we can rule out the possibility that the killer escaped through the doors near the victim. I admit there are many possibilities - for example, Maggie O'Connell could be an accomplice. I'm just suggesting this as a possibility, not an opinion. At least in my opinion, the killer wouldn't take the risk of being seen leaving through those side doors. Moreover, leaving in an unusual way at an unusual time would be highly noticeable, especially during the second act when almost no one left. Besides, the killer couldn't have known that O'Connell would be neglecting her duties - unless she was an accomplice. Since this case was carefully planned - we must admit, the evidence suggests so - the killer might have given up the idea of escaping through the side doors.\" [717] \"The escape route, I think, is only one left, and that is the main entrance. However, the ticket taker and the doorkeeper outside confirmed that no one left the theater during the second act. Of course, except for the boy selling oranges, who was irrelevant.\" [718] \"All the exits were either guarded or locked, and from 9:35, there was someone in the alley - first Linnie, then Eleanor, then the male usher - Johnny Thurston - and then the police - these facts, along with my questioning and investigation, gentlemen. The inevitable conclusion is that the killer has been in the theater since the body was found!\" [719] After Ellery finished speaking, the room fell silent. \"By the way,\" Ellery added calmly, \"I remember asking the ushers if they saw anyone leave their seats during the second act, and they all said they couldn't remember anyone changing seats.\" [720] Quin absent-mindedly took a pinch of snuff. \"Well done - a reasonable deduction, my son. But ultimately, it's nothing surprising or decisive. Even if the killer was still in the theater, how can we catch him?\" [721] \"He didn't say you could catch him,\" said Sampson, smiling. \"Don't be so sensitive, old friend; nobody will say you were derelict in your duty during the investigation. As far as I know, you handled the whole thing very well tonight.\" [722] Quinn muttered, \"I admit, my not having thoroughly questioned the matter of the door bothers me a bit. But even if the murderer might have left immediately after the crime, I would still have asked the same question, hoping he would still be in the theater.\" [723] \"But, father\u2014of course!\" Ellery said solemnly, \"You have so many things to attend to, and my task is simply to sit back and observe, like Socrates.\" [724] \"So far, how have the people who have been surveyed been doing?\" Sampson curiously asked. [725] \"Hmm, how are they?\" Eleanor questioned, \"We can't seem to get a clear answer from either talking to them or observing their actions. We caught Parsons Johnny, the villain, but it's clear he was just a fan of the show, looking for some juicy gossip about his colleagues. As for Maggie O'Connell, she's suspicious, but we can't make any conclusions at this stage. She could be an accomplice, innocent, or just careless \u2013 she could play any role. Then there's William Pusack, who discovered Field. But have you noticed how slow-witted he is? And Benjamin Morgan \u2013 he's where we found the potential lead. But what can we make of his behavior tonight? Yes, the letter and the free ticket do sound odd, as anyone could have written that letter, even Morgan himself. I must also remember his public threat to kill Field; and the animosity between them, although the reason is unclear, has been going on for two years. Finally, there's Miss Frances Eves-Pope. Unfortunately, I wasn't present when you spoke with her. However, the fact remains that we found her handbag in the deceased's pocket \u2013 isn't that interesting? Could you explain that, please?\" [726] \"So far, that's the situation,\" Ellery continued regretfully. \"We have too many doubts from tonight's case, but too few facts.\" [727] \"So far, son,\" said Quin calmly. \"Your reasoning is flawless. But you've forgotten one important thing\u2014the empty seats. And another surprising fact\u2014the ticket stub of Field, and the only one that could possibly belong to the killer\u2014I mean the LL30 left one that Flint found\u2014these two tickets don't match. That is, the tear marks on the tickets show that the ticket taker checked these two tickets at different times!\" [728] \"Right,\" said Ellery. \"But let's put that aside for now and talk about Field's hat.\" [729] \"That hat\u2014uh, what do you think?\" Quin asked curiously. [730] \"Well, first, we're quite sure the hat didn't disappear by accident. Jesse Lynch saw the dead man's hat on his knees about ten minutes after the second act started. But now the hat is gone, and the only logical explanation is that the killer took it. Now\u2014no matter where the hat is\u2014we can immediately draw one of two conclusions: either the hat itself is connected to the case, and leaving it behind might reveal the killer's identity. We don't know what the connection is yet; or, the hat may contain something the killer wants. You might ask, why didn't the killer take what was inside and leave the hat behind? If this assumption is correct, it may be because he didn't have enough time to take it out, or he didn't know how to take it out, so he took the hat to study it later. Agree with my reasoning so far?\" [731] The district attorney nodded slowly. Quincannon sat motionless, a look of confusion in his eyes. [732] \"Now let's think about what might be in the hat. Ellery continued, wiping his glasses vigorously. \"Based on the size, shape, and volume of the hat, our range of possibilities is not very broad. What could be in a hat? Some of the things I can think of are mainly: some document, jewelry, money, or any other valuable small item that wouldn't be easily found in the hat. Obviously, this mysterious thing couldn't be in the top of the hat, because as soon as he took it off, it would fall out. So, we can conclude that whatever it is, it should be inside the hat. This immediately narrows down the possibilities. Solid large items can be ruled out. It could be jewelry, money, or documents. I think, through our understanding of Flint, we can rule out jewelry. If he carried something important, it would probably be related to his profession. [733] \"In the preliminary analysis of the missing hat, there is one more thing we need to consider. And, gentlemen, this should be crucial to our case before we solve it, that is, whether the killer knew before the murder that he had to take the hat away. In other words, did the killer know the importance of the hat, no matter how important it was? In my opinion, whether it's from the facts or from logical reasoning, it can be shown that the killer did not know the importance of the hat beforehand. [734] \"Please follow my line of thinking... Since Flint's hat is missing and no other hat was found in his place, it's clear that the hat was taken, and that's a fact. You should agree, as I pointed out earlier, that the killer is the most likely person to take the hat. Good! Let's put aside why he took the hat for now, we still have two choices: one, the killer knew beforehand that he had to take the hat; two, he didn't know beforehand that he had to take the hat. Let's examine the first possibility. If he knew beforehand, we can logically assume that the killer should have brought another hat to the theater to replace Flint's hat, instead of taking the victim's hat directly, leaving a clear clue. Bringing a replacement hat would be safer. And it shouldn't be difficult for the killer to find such a hat, because if he knew its importance beforehand, he would have further investigated the dimensions, style, and other details of Flint's hat. But we don't find a replacement hat here. We have every reason to believe that such a well-planned case should have had a replacement hat. But it's not there, so our only conclusion is that the killer didn't know the importance of the hat beforehand; otherwise, he would have taken preventive measures and left a hat. That way, the police would never know the importance of Flint's hat. [735] \"One more thing to confirm is that even if the killer had some unknown reason not to leave a replacement hat, he would have cut the hat open and\n[736] The district attorney stared at Ellery, his lips pressed together. Detective Quinn appeared to be dozing, his hand hovering between his snuff box and his nose. [737] \"What do you mean, Ellery?\" Sampson asked. \"Why is it so important that the killer didn't know the significance of the hat?\" [738] Ellery smiled. \"The murder occurred during the second act, and I wanted to use that to establish that if the killer didn't know the significance of the hat beforehand, he couldn't have planned the murder during the first intermission... Of course, Fielding's hat could appear somewhere in the theater, and once found, all these theories would be invalid. But... I don't think it will... \"\n[739] \"You've done some basic analysis, but it sounds logical,\" Sampson commended. \"You should become a lawyer.\" [740] \"You're no match for the Quinn family in terms of intellect,\" the old man suddenly broke into a grin. \"But I have to attend to something else related to the hat mystery. Ellery, have you noticed that the tailor's name is sewn onto Fielding's coat?\" [741] \"Let's do it,\" Ellery grinned, pulling out a small notebook from his coat pocket and flipping it open to a blank page. \"Brown Brothers, gentlemen\u2014that's it.\" [742] \"Alright, I'll have Wiley check it out first thing in the morning,\" Quine said. \"You must have realized that Mr. Field's clothing is of high quality. That evening suit would be worth around $300 if bought. Brown Bros. is a renowned tailor, so the price is high. Another relevant point is: all the clothes on the deceased were made by the same manufacturer. For wealthy people, this is nothing special; Brown's would make a complete outfit from head to toe to make the customer stand out. We can be more certain\u2014\"\n[743] \"Mr. Field's hat was also bought there!\" Sampson exclaimed, his face lighting up with a sense of discovery. [744] \"Absolutely correct, Tacitus,\" Quine said, grinning. \"Wiley's job is to verify the clothes, and if possible, get a replica of the same hat Mr. Field wore tonight. I'm eager to see this hat.\" [745] Sampson coughed and stood up. \"I think I should go back to bed,\" he said. \"The only reason I came here was to make sure you didn't arrest the mayor. Buddy, if my friend gets angry, my ears won't be quiet.\" [746] Quine looked up at him, smiling mockingly. \"Before you leave, Henry, tell me your position on this. I know I used high-pressure tactics tonight, but you must have realized it was necessary. Are you going to send someone from your side to assist in the investigation?\" [747] Sampson glared at him. \"When did you start feeling like I'm not satisfied with your investigation? You old fart!\" he roared. \"I've never questioned you, and I don't want to now. If you can't solve this, I don't think anyone else on my side can. My dear Quine, go ahead and do what you need to do. If you need to, you can detain half of New York, and I'll support you.\" [748] \"Thank you, Henry,\" Quine said. \"I just wanted to confirm. Now, since you're so nice, wait and see me do my thing.\" [749] He walked across the room, into the reception area, and called out to the theater from the corridor. \"Mr. Pensel, can you come here for a moment?\" [750] He came back, smiling slightly, as the dark-skinned Pensel followed behind him. [751] \"Mr. Poulson, this is District Attorney Sampson,\" Quinn introduced. They shook hands. \"Now, Mr. Poulson, you have one more thing to do before you can go rest. I want you to close the theater and not let any mice in.\" [752] Poulson's face paled. Sampson shrugged, as if he no longer cared about the matter, and Ellery nodded in agreement. [753] \"But\u2014but officer, we've been having a full house for our performances!\" the short manager moaned. \"Is it really necessary to do this?\" [754] \"Absolutely necessary, my dear fellow,\" the officer replied calmly. \"I'll have two men patrolling around the theater.\" [755] Poulson twisted his hands, casting a glance at Sampson. But the district attorney was turned away, admiring a painting on the wall. [756] \"Oh, that's terrible, Officer!\" Pincus sighed. \"Gordon Davis, the producer, will be on my case about this... But, of course\u2014if you say so, I'll do it.\" [757] \"Ah, don't be so glum, my friend,\" Quinn said, his tone more soothing. \"Through this, your theater's reputation will skyrocket. Once it reopens, you'll have to expand. Anyway, I won't keep your theater closed for too long. I'll give the necessary orders to my men outside, and you can quietly tell them what you need to do when you're done with your daily tasks tonight. You can go home after that. I'll tell you when the theater can reopen in a few days.\" [758] Pincus shook his head sadly and left after shaking hands with them. Sampson immediately turned to Quinn. \"My goodness, Quinn, that's a bit much! Why are you closing the theater? You've searched it top to bottom, haven't you?\" [759] \"But, Henry,\" Quinn said slowly, \"the hat is still missing. We've searched everyone who left the theater, and everyone has only one hat. That means the hat we're looking for must be somewhere here. If it is, I won't let anyone have the chance to come in and take it. I'll be the one to take it if it's here.\" [760] Sampson nodded. As the three of them left the office and entered the nearly empty theater, Ellery frowned with concern. Occasionally, a busy figure could be seen bending over the seats, carefully inspecting the ground. People kept coming and going from the front boxes. Chief Wiley stood by the main entrance, talking in a low voice with Pigott and Haggstrom. Detective Flint was directing some