n the ground. I didn't touch him, but I felt his chest and found no heartbeat. To confirm if he was dead, I called a doctor \u2013 a Mr. Stuttgart \u2013 to help me check... \"\n[81] Inspector Quin stood up abruptly, tilting his head like a parrot. \"Great,\" he said, \"great, Doralee. I'll ask Stuttgart later. What happened next?\" he asked. [82] \"Then,\" the policeman continued, \"I sent the usheress down this corridor to find Mr. Poulsen, Louis Poulsen - the manager over there...\"\n[83] Inspector Quin nodded as he watched Poulsen talking to Nelson a few feet away. [84] \"That's Poulsen you're talking about. Okay, okay... Ellery! Did you get my message?\" [85] He pushed Poulsen aside (Poulsen apologized and stepped back), and approached a tall young man who was entering through the door, taking a leisurely look around. The old man took the young man's arm. [86] \"Didn't bother you too much, son? Which bookstore did you visit tonight? Ellery, I'm so glad you came! I reached into my pocket, took out a snuff box, and inhaled deeply \u2014 too deeply, causing a sneeze \u2014 and then looked up at my son's face. [87] \"In fact,\" Ellery Quinn impatiently turned his eyes, \"I can't reciprocate your compliments. You dragged me out of a paradise for book lovers. I was just convincing the owner to sell me the first edition of Faulkner's novel, a priceless treasure, and I was planning to borrow money from you at the headquarters. I called you over there \u2014 so I came. A Faulkner novel \u2014 uh, never mind, I'll buy it tomorrow.\" [88] The officer chuckled. \"If you tell me you found an antique snuff box, I might be interested. Looks like \u2014 come on, we seem to have work to do tonight.\" [89] The old man tugged at his son's coat sleeve, walking towards a small group of people on the left. Ellery Quinn was six inches taller than his father, with hair cut neatly along the contours of his face, reaching his shoulders, and swaying rhythmically with his movements. He wo