"Looking for some small things?\" [881] \"You're quite the quick dresser for a woman, Mrs. Lasso,\" complimented the officer. \"Getting ready to go home?\" [882] She quickly glanced at Quin. \"Of course,\" she answered, looking away. [883] \"So you live at -\"\n[884] She gave the address of a Greenwich Village McDougal Street. [885] \"Thank you,\" said Quin politely, noting it down. She began to walk through the room. \"Oh, Mrs. Lasso!\" She turned around. \"Before you leave - perhaps you can tell us something about Field's drinking habits. Do you think he's a heavy drinker?\" [886] She smiled brightly. \"Well, it's a bit of both. I've seen Fielder drink for half the night and still be as\u2014as sober as a priest. But sometimes he'd only have a few drinks and be as drunk as a skunk. It depends\u2014you don't understand, do you?\" [887] \"Well, many people are like that,\" the officer whispered, \"I don't mean to pry, Mrs. Roxo, but perhaps you know where his liquor comes from?\" [888] She stopped smiling immediately, her face contorted with anger. \"Who do you take me for?\" she asked. \"I don't know, and even if I did, I wouldn't tell you. There are many hardworking moonshiners out there who are more honorable than the people trying to catch them, believe me!\" [889] \"There are many such people in the world, Mrs. Roxo,\" Quin consoled. \"But, my dear,\" he continued gently, \"when the time comes to need that information, I'm sure you'll tell me, right?\" [890] Mrs. Roxo didn't respond. \"Well, I think that's all for today, Mrs. Roxo. Please don't leave town, okay? We might need your testimony soon.\" [891] \"Mrs. Roxbury!\" Quinny suddenly exclaimed sharply. She had already reached for the doorknob with her gloved hand, but she turned around, her smile gone. \"Do you know what Benjamin Morgan has been up to since he split from Field?\" [892] After a moment's hesitation, she replied. \"Who is he?\" she asked, her eyebrows furrowed tightly. [893] Quinny stood firmly on the carpet. He said sadly, \"Never mind. Goodbye.\" He turned around and faced away from her. The door slammed shut. After a while, Hagstrom left, and Piggott, Ellery, and Quinny remained in the apartment. [894] The three of them seemed to be struck by an idea and rushed into the bedroom. The room was just as they had left it. The bed was a mess, and Mrs. Roxbury's nightgown and day clothes were scattered on the floor. Quinny opened the bedroom closet door. \"Ah!\" Ellery said. \"He's quite the connoisseur of clothes, isn't he? A bit like the dandy of Sycamore Street, Brummel.\" They searched the entire closet but found nothing. Ellery leaned over to look at the shelves above. \"No hat\u2014no cane\u2014and that's that,\" he said with a smirk. Piggott went into the small kitchen and came out with a half-empty box of liquor. [895] Ellery and Quinny both bent over the liquor box. The officer carefully removed the cork and sniffed it, then handed the bottle to Piggott. Piggott also sniffed it carefully, imitating his superior. [896] \"Seems fine, smells fine,\" Piggott said. \"But after last night, I don't want to taste this.\" [897] \"Your caution is very reasonable,\" Ellery chuckled. \"But if you change your mind, pray to Bacchus, Piggott, and let him know: Ah, wine, if you have no other name, let us call you death.\" [898] \"I'll take this liquor for analysis,\" Quinn whispered. \"A blend of Scotch and rye, the label looks real. But it's hard to say...\"\n[899] Suddenly, Ellery grabbed his father's arm, leaning forward tensely. The three of them stood frozen in place. [900] A barely audible scratching sound could be heard coming from the hallway. [901] Piggott quickly crossed the living room and hid in the hallway. Quin and Ellery hid in the bedroom, out of sight of the incoming person. [902] There was silence all around, except for the scratching sound from outside. The newcomer seemed to have difficulty using the key. Suddenly, the bolt in the lock clicked back, and the door opened, then slammed shut immediately. [903] A muffled shout was heard, a hoarse sound like a bull, along with Piggott's muffled curses and the sound of dragging footsteps. Ellery and his father quickly crossed the living room and joined Piggott in the hallway. [904] Piggott struggled in the arms of a tall, muscular man dressed in black. A suitcase lay on the floor, seemingly thrown there during the struggle. As Ellery approached the two arguing men, a newspaper fluttered down from the air. [905] With the three of them working together, they finally subdued the intruder. Finally, he lay panting on the floor, with Piggott's arm firmly pressing down on his chest. [906] The officer leaned down, curiously studying the man's flushed face, and asked in a gentle tone, \"Who are you, sir?\" [907] Chapter 9: The Mysterious Michaelis\n[908] The intruder clumsily stood up. He was tall but seemed heavyset; his face was stern, but his eyes lacked any spark. There was nothing particularly remarkable about his appearance or demeanor. If anything, it was his ordinary appearance and behavior that seemed intentional in erasing all his personality traits. [909] \"What does this violence mean?\" he asked in a low voice. But even his voice sounded dull and boring. [910] Quin turned to Piggott. \"What happened?\" he asked sternly. [911] \"I was standing behind the door, officer,\" Piggott said, panting and still out of breath, \"when this wildcat came in, and I touched his arm, and he pounced on me like a tiger. He pushed my face\u2014he got a punch in, officer...and he tried to run out the door again.\" [912] Quinn nodded like a judge. The newcomer spoke in a calm tone, \"He's lying, sir. He pounced on me, and I just defended myself.\" [913] \"Alright, alright,\" Quinn murmured, \"this will never get sorted out...\"\n[914] The door suddenly opened, and Johnson, the investigator, stood in the doorway. He pulled the officer aside. \"Wiley just sent me over to see if you needed any help, officer...when I came up, I saw this guy. I'm not sure, but I felt like he was casing the area, so I followed him.\" [915] Quinn nodded vigorously. \"I'm glad you came\u2014I need your help.\" He muttered, then gestured to the others, leading the way into the living room. [916] \"Now, my friend,\" he said tersely to the burly intruder, \"that's enough. Who are you, and what are you doing here?\" [917] \"I'm Charles Michaels\u2014sir. I'm Montgomery Field's personal servant.\" [918] The officer's eyes narrowed. The man's behavior had subtly changed. He still looked blank and his demeanor remained unchanged, but the old man felt a qualitative change. He quickly glanced at Ellery, confirming his suspicions through his son's eyes. [919] \"Is that so?\" the officer asked calmly, \"Personal servant, ah? Where were you going with that suitcase this morning?\" [920] His hand suddenly pointed at the handbag. It was a black cheap leather bag that Piggott had picked up from the hallway and brought into the living room. Ellery suddenly walked towards the hallway, then bent down and picked something up. \"What?\" Michaels said, his voice wavering, \"That's mine, sir. I was going to go on vacation this morning. I was supposed to come here to pick up my paycheck from Mr. Field before I left.\" [921] The old man's eyes flashed. He had finally figured out where Michaels had changed! Although his expression and overall demeanor remained the same, his voice and pronunciation had clearly changed. [922] \"So, you had an appointment with Field this morning to pick up the check?\" the officer murmured. \"I remember, it's funny!\u201d\n[923] A look of surprise flashed across Michaels' face, but it was quickly gone. \"What - what happened to Field?\" he asked.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 8:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nIf Stephen Barry and Field knew each other, and Frances was unaware, the possible link might be Stephen Barry. For example, if Field had an appointment with the actor on Monday evening and suddenly saw Frances, he might have dared to approach her, especially if the topic between him and Barry was related to her. As for why she recognized her - thousands of people who read the daily newspaper are familiar with her face - she is a young socialite who is often photographed. Field, with his comprehensive business methods, naturally knew her appearance and appearance... But let's go back to this triangle relationship - Field, Frances, Barry - I will explain in detail later. Through photos and other news channels, everyone knows that Barry is engaged to Frances and is her fianc\u00e9. You must have realized that, in this case, among the actors, no one else can answer this question: why did Field approach Frances? [2164] \"There is another puzzling factor about Frances - her purse was found in Field's clothes - a reasonable explanation is that, when the inebriated lawyer approached her, she dropped it out of nervousness. Later, Jesse Lynch's testimony also corroborated this, as his testimony suggested that he saw Field pick up Frances' purse. Poor girl - I feel sorry for her.\" The officer sighed. [2165] \"Let's go back to the hat\u2014you'll notice we've always been mentioning that damn hat.\" Quin paused, then continued, \"I've never seen a case where a single factor has played a part in every aspect of an investigation\u2026Now, pay attention to this: out of all the cast members, Barry was the only one who left the Roman Theater on Monday night wearing a tuxedo and a top hat. On Monday night, Ellery noticed as people were leaving the theater that everyone else in the cast was dressed in casual attire; in fact, later, when he was in Pencroft's office, he mentioned this to Sampson and me, but we didn't fully realize its significance at the time\u2026Therefore, Barry was the only cast member who could have taken Field's hat. By using Ellery's reasoning about the hat, we can now cut through the fog and conclude that Barry is the suspect. [2166] \"The next step we need to do is to see the performance ourselves. That evening - Thursday, Ellery made a decisive inference. You'll understand why. We wanted to see if Barry had time to commit the crime during the second act to confirm our conclusion. It was surprising that out of all the cast members, only Barry had the time. He left the stage at 9:20 - he went on stage, and then left almost immediately - and didn't return until 9:50, staying on stage until the act ended. This was an undeniable part of the fixed schedule. The other actors were either on stage throughout or had very short intervals between going on and off stage. This means that on Thursday night, which was five days ago, we solved the mystery - the whole case took nine days to solve, but figuring out the identity of the killer and bringing him to justice were two different things. You'll understand in a moment.\" [2167] \"The killer wouldn't be able to enter until around 9:30, which explains why the edges torn off of LL32 left and LL30 left are not consistent. You know, Field and Barry had to come in at different times. Field wouldn't be able to come in with Barry, or even be too obviously late \u2013 it was too important for Barry to keep the secret, and Field understood, or he thought he understood the necessity of adhering to the rules of secrecy.\" [2168] \"When we determined Barry to be a suspect on Thursday evening, we decided to subtly interrogate the other members of the cast and the staff behind the scenes. Of course, we wanted to find out if anyone had truly seen Barry leave or return. Coincidentally, no one had. Everyone was either busy with the performance, changing costumes, or working backstage. This investigation was conducted after the show ended that night and Barry had left the theater. This approach turned out to be quite effective. [2169] \"We borrowed a seating chart from Poulson. With this chart, along with a quick check of the left aisle after the second act on Thursday night, and the layout of the dressing rooms on stage \u2013 we can figure out how the killer did it.\" [2170] Sampson was visibly excited. \"I've been wrestling with this for quite some time,\" he admitted, \"after all, Field isn't a fool. This Barry must be a genius, Kwai, how did he do it?\" [2171] \"Once the mystery is unraveled, any riddle is simple,\" the detective answered. \"After 9:20, Barry had free time, so he quickly went back to the dressing room, did a thorough disguise on his face, put on a cloak, and a high hat, which was part of his costume - you all remember, he was wearing evening dress - and then slipped out of the dressing room and into the alley. Of course, you don't know the layout of the theater. There are many floor levels facing the left alley on the backstage side of the theater, which are dressing rooms. Barry's dressing room was on the ground floor, with a door facing the alley. There is an iron staircase leading down to the sidewalk. [2172] \"He left the dressing room through this door, slipped through the dark alley when the side door was closed during the second act. He sneaked out onto the street when no one was guarding the end of the alley - luckily for him, Jesse Lynch and his 'girl' hadn't arrived yet. Then he walked boldly into the theater as if he were late. He showed his ticket at the door - LL30 left, wrapped in a cloak, of course disguised very well. He entered the theater and deliberately threw away the stub. This was a wise move on his part, as he thought that if they found the stub there, it would only arouse suspicion in a certain audience member, not on the stage. Moreover, if his plan failed, he would be searched thoroughly later, and the stub found on him would be irrefutable evidence. Ultimately, he thought his actions would not only divert attention but also protect him. [2173] \"But how did he find his seat without a usher? If there had been an usher, he would have been seen. \"The detective answered, \"He didn't intend to avoid the usher. Since the performance was going on, the theater was dark, and he naturally hoped that the usher hadn't arrived yet and sat in the last row closest to the door. Even if the usher arrived before him and led him to his seat, with his disguise and the darkness of the theater, he wouldn't be recognized. Therefore, in the worst case, others would only remember that a man came in during the second act of the performance, and even his general outline would be difficult to describe. Coincidentally, no one approached him because Maggie O'Connell was luckily sitting next to her sweetheart. So he could sit next to Field without being noticed.\" [2174] \"But how did he find his seat without an usher? If there had been an usher, he would have been seen.\" Croaning disbelievingly. [2175] \"He had no intention of avoiding the usher,\" the officer replied, \"since it was during the performance and the theater was dimly lit, he naturally hoped the usher had not yet arrived and was seated in the back row closest to the entrance. However, even if the usher arrived before him and led him to his seat, due to his good disguise and the dark theater, he would not be recognized. Therefore, in the worst-case scenario, others' recollections of him would be of a man who entered during the second act, with a body outline that was barely describable. Coincidentally, no one approached him because Marcie O'Connell was fortunately sitting next to her sweetheart. Thus, he was able to sit quietly next to Field without being noticed. [2176] \"Please remember, what I just said is not a deduction or investigation result. We don't have the ability to discover these facts. Barry's confession last night revealed these details... Once we knew the criminal was Barry, we could deduce the whole process... If you know the criminal, it's simple and normal. However, this is not necessary for solving the case. Does this sound like an excuse for Ellery or me? Hmmph!\" The old man forced a smile. [2177] \"He had a well-planned plan when he sat next to Field,\" the officer cleared his throat, continuing. \"He had to strictly follow the schedule and not waste a minute. Moreover, Field knew that Barry had to return, so he didn't delay. Barry told us that he thought it would be difficult to deal with Field, but it wasn't as difficult as he thought. Field was amiable and accepted Barry's suggestion, perhaps because he had drunk too much and wanted to get that large sum of money quickly. [2178] \"Barry first asked for the documents. Field cunningly demanded money before showing the documents, and Barry showed him a wallet that was obviously full of real bills. The theater was very dark, and Barry didn't take out the bills. In fact, these were the bills used in the performance on stage. Barry hinted at the bills, but he wanted to check the documents first before paying. Field was probably expecting this. We know that Barry is a successful actor, and his training on stage allows him to handle difficult situations with ease...\n[2179] \"Imagine his reaction when Field reached under his seat and pulled out his high hat. Barry confessed that at the time, Field said, 'Didn't you expect me to hide the documents here? In fact, I specifically use this hat to store your records. Look - here is your name.' He said this while flipping the hatband over! Barry saw his name written in ink on the inside of the hatband with his flashlight. [2180] \"Imagine his brain's reaction at that moment. His meticulous plan was about to be ruined by this hat. If his hat had been checked when his body was found - of course, it would have been checked - the name 'Stephen Barry' on the hatband would have been the ironclad evidence... Barry didn't have time to remove the hatband. First, he didn't have a knife - unfortunately\n[2181] \"Sensitive mind, Henry, sensitive mind,\" Quinn said gravely, \"many have gone to the gallows... just as he was about to take the hat, he realized he couldn't leave his own there. For one, his hat was a fedora \u2013 a foldable, wide-brimmed hat \u2013 but more importantly, it bore the name of the costume supplier, L\u00e9on Broun. You can see, this would immediately point to someone in the production \u2013 which was exactly what he wanted to avoid. He told me, at the time and for some time after, he thought the police could only infer that something valuable was hidden in the hat. He couldn't fathom how this line of investigation would implicate him. When I explained to him that Ellery simply made deductions based on the fact of the lost hat, he was completely astounded... Now you understand, his only fundamental flaw in the commission of the crime was not negligence or error, but the unforeseen occurrence that forced him to act, which in turn set off a chain reaction. If Barry's name hadn't been on Field's hat, I wouldn't have had any suspicions, and until today, he would have been a free man with no suspicion. The police files would have added another unsolved murder case. [2182] \"As you can imagine, these thoughts flashed through his mind in a moment, not as long as I've described. He knew what he had to do, and his plans had to be adjusted immediately due to the new developments... After Field retrieved the documents from the hat, Barry quickly checked them under the watchful gaze of the lawyer. He used the same mini flashlight, which was dimmed due to the obstruction from the two of them. The documents appeared to be well-organized and complete. However, Barry didn't spend much time on them at that moment. He lifted his head and said, \"Seems like everything's here, damn it\u2014\" in a natural tone, as if they were already reconciled enemies, and he was a magnanimous person. Field didn't understand the true meaning of this statement. Barry reached into his pocket \u2013 the flashlight was off at that time \u2013 and appeared to be nervous, taking a gulp of the premium whisky from the bottle. He then seemed to realize he was being impolite and asked Field if he would like to take a sip to celebrate the successful transaction. Seeing Barry drink from the bottle, Field no longer had any suspicions. In fact, he probably never imagined that Barry would try to eliminate him. Barry handed him the bottle...\n[2183] \"But it wasn't the same bottle. Under the cover of darkness, he took out two bottles - the one he drank from was taken out of his left back pocket, while the one he gave to Field was taken out of his right back pocket. It was simple - it was dark there, and the lawyer was already drunk, so it was even simpler. The bottle trick worked. But Barry was thorough. He had a syringe filled with poison in his pocket. If Field refused to drink, Barry was prepared to inject the needle into Field's arm or thigh. He had an injection needle given to him by a doctor years ago. Barry had suffered from neurasthenia but could not undergo normal treatment by the doctor because he had to perform with the troupe in various places. Since the syringe was old, its origin could not be traced; Barry was also prepared if Field did not drink. You see - even in such details, his plan was flawless...\"\n[2184] \"The bottle of Scotch that Field drank contained high-quality whiskey, that's true, but it was heavily laced with tetraethyl lead. The strong smell of the liquor masked the subtle ether taste of the poison; Field had taken a large gulp before realizing something was amiss - if he had realized at all. [2185] \"He handed the bottle back to Barry without a second thought, and Barry pocketed it, saying, 'I think I need to look over these documents more closely - I don't trust you, Field...' Field was by now so dull-witted that he simply nodded his head blankly, then collapsed onto his seat. Barry truly examined the documents, but all the while kept an eagle's eye on Field with the corner of his eye. After about five minutes, he saw that Field was finished - completely finished. He wasn't completely unconscious, but he was close; his face contorted, and he couldn't breathe. He seemed unable to move or cry out. Of course, in his agony, he forgot all about Barry, and soon he was completely unconscious. When he spoke his last words to Basker, it was merely the dying flicker of a flame...\n[2186] \"Barry looked at his watch. It was 9:40, he had only been with Field for ten minutes. He had to be back on stage at 9:50. He decided to wait another three minutes - this took less time than he had anticipated - to ensure that Field wouldn't make any more noise. At 9:43, Field was in agony and on the brink of death, Barry picked up Field's hat, quickly removed his own hat and tucked it under his cloak, stood up. He was familiar with the route. He stuck to the wall, carefully and inconspicuously making his way along the aisle to the back of the left box. The play was at its climax, and everyone was focused on the stage, none of them noticing him. [2187] \"Behind the box, he tore off the wig, quickly adjusted his makeup, and walked through the stage door. This door led to a narrow passage, which led to a corridor, which then turned into different areas of the stage area. His dressing room was only a few feet away from the entrance of the corridor. He slipped in, tossing his prop hat into his ordinary items, pouring the remaining poison from the bottle into the sink, and washing the bottle. He poured the remaining liquid from the syringe into the toilet, and put away the cleaned needle. If he was caught - what did it matter? He had a valid reason for having the syringe, and besides, he hadn't used it in the murder case... He was now ready to go on stage, calm, happy, and confident, but also slightly annoyed. At exactly 9:50, he was called to the stage. He walked up to the stage, and at 9:55, there was a shout from the front row of the auditorium... \"\n[2188] \"Discuss your complex plans,\" said Sampson suddenly. [2189] \"It's not as complicated as it first sounded,\" the officer replied, \"Remember, Barry is a very intelligent and accomplished young man, especially an excellent actor. Only an experienced actor could pull off a plan like this. After all, the process is simple; the hardest part is sticking to the schedule. If he was seen, he would have been disguised. The most dangerous part of his plan was \u2013 how to escape when he walked down the aisle, through the backstage entrance to the stage, while the ushers were there. He had observed the ushers while sitting next to Field. Of course, he knew that due to the nature of the performance, the ushers would be mostly faithful to their duties, and he counted on his disguise and syringe to help him through any unexpected situations. However, Maggie O'Connell did not do her job properly, so that was to his advantage. He proudly told me last night that he had prepared for emergencies... As for the backstage entrance, he knew from experience that everyone would be on stage and the technicians would be busy in their positions at that time... Remember, he planned the crime with the exact conditions in mind. If there were any dangers and uncertainties \u2013 well, isn't that the nature of an adventure? \u2013 he asked me with a smile last night; I had to admit that, if not for this, I would greatly admire his style.\" [2190] The officer uneasily shifted the topic. \"I hope I've made it clear how Barry committed the crime. As for our investigation... besides the reasoning about the hat and knowing the identity of the killer, we still know nothing about the exact details of the crime. If you remember the evidence we gathered on Thursday night, you will understand that there was nothing to go on. What we hope most is that there is some clue in the files we need to find that is related to Barry. Even then, it's not enough... so the next step, the officer sighed, \"is to find the secret compartment on the canopy of the bed in Field's apartment. This work has been done by Ellery from beginning to end. We found that Field did not have a safe, no mailbox, no other residence, no intimate neighbors or business partners, and those files were not in his office. After elimination, Ellery insisted that these files were in Field's room. You know how the search ended - completely through Ellery's genius reasoning. We found Morgan's files, discovered the material needed by Croning about the mafia activities - by the way, Tim, I keenly noticed what happened when we started the big clean-up - we finally found a bundle of miscellaneous files, including Michaels and Barry's... Tim, you will remember, Ellery deduced from handwriting analysis that we might find Barry's original files - and indeed we did. [2191] \"Mickles' case was quite interesting.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[924] \"Rest in peace, Mr. Moseby,\" Ellery's laughter echoed from the foyer. He returned to the living room, waving the newspaper Michaels had dropped during his fight with Pugott. \"Really, enough, old chap, it's too much. That's your newspaper. When I picked it up, the first thing I saw was the black headline describing Field's accident, filling the front page. Have you not seen this report?\" [925] Michaels stared blankly at Ellery and the newspaper. But he lowered his eyes and muttered, \"I haven't had a chance to read the paper this morning, sir. What happened to Field?\" [926] The officer snorted. \"Field was murdered, Michaels, and you knew it all along.\" [927] \"But I truly didn't, I tell you, sir,\" the butler respectfully countered. [928] \"Don't lie to us!\" Quincannon growled. \"Tell us why you're here, or you'll have plenty of time to tell the truth once you're locked up in jail.\" [929] Michaels calmly looked at the old man. \"I'm telling the truth, sir. Mr. Field told me last night to come here this morning to pick up the check. That's all I know.\" [930] \"You were supposed to meet him here?\" [931] \"Yes, sir.\" [932] \"So why did you forget to ring the bell and use the key, as if you never expected anyone to be here, my friend?\" Quin asked. [933] \"The bell?\" The butler's eyes widened. \"I always use the key, sir. If I can open the door myself, I never disturb Mr. Field.\" [934] \"Why didn't Field give you the check yesterday?\" the officer barked. [935] \"I think he didn't have his checkbook with him, sir.\" [936] Quin raised an eyebrow. \"Your imagination isn't very rich, Michaels. When was the last time you saw Field last night?\" [937] \"Around seven o'clock, sir,\" Michaels answered promptly, \"I don't live in the apartment. It's too small, and Mr. Field values his privacy. I usually come over early to make breakfast for him, get the bathwater ready, and lay out his clothes. Then, after he goes to the office, I tidy up the room, and the rest of the time is mine until dinner. I come back at five to prepare dinner for Mr. Field. If he tells me he's going out to eat, I help him get dressed. Then my work for the evening is done... Yesterday, after I put everything away for him, he told me about the check.\" [938] \"Not a very strenuous schedule,\" Ellery murmured, \"What did you put away for Field last night, Michaels?\" [939] The man faced Ellery with respect. \"His undergarments, sir; his socks, evening shoes, crisp shirt, cufflinks, collar, white tie, tuxedo, cape, hat\u2014\"\n[940] \"Ah, yes\u2014his hat,\" Quin interjected, \"What kind of hat, Michaels?\" [941] \"The same hat he usually wears, sir,\" Michael answered, \"It's very expensive, I think, Brown Brothers brand.\" [942] Queequeg tapped the arm of his chair with his finger lazily. \"Tell me, Michael,\" he said, \"What did you do after you left here last night - around seven o'clock?\" [943] \"I went home, sir. I was so tired after packing my bags that I just ate something and went to bed - I think I went to bed around nine-thirty, sir.\" He added innocently. [944] \"Where do you live?\" Michael gave the address of 146 East 1st Street in the Bronx. \"I know... Does anyone often come to visit Field here?\" [945] Michael frowned slightly, but remained polite. \"I find it hard to say, Mr. Field is not the kind of person you think he is. And I'm not here in the evenings, so I don't know who might come after I leave. But -\"\n[946] \"But what?\" [947] \"There's a lady, sir...,\" Michaelson hesitated, looking serious. \"In this situation, I don't want to mention anyone's name\u2014\"\n[948] \"What's her name?\" [949] \"Well, sir\u2014this is a bit ungentlemanly... Lasso, Mrs. Angela Lasso, that's her name.\" Michaelson answered. [950] \"How long has Mr. Field known Mrs. Lasso?\" [951] \"A few months, sir. I believe Mr. Field met her at a gathering in Greenwich Village.\" [952] \"I see. Did they get engaged?\" [953] Michael looked a bit embarrassed. \"You could say so, sir, although their engagement was not very formal -\"\n[954] There was a pause, and the officer continued to ask, \"How long have you been employed by Mr. Field?\" [955] \"Almost three years, sir.\" [956] Quinn changed the subject, turning to a new line of questioning. He asked Michaelson about whether Field liked going to the theater, his financial situation, and his drinking habits. In these details, Michaelson confirmed what Mrs. Rask had said, but did not reveal anything new. [957] \"You said you've been working for Field for about three years now,\" Quinn said, sitting back in his chair. \"How did you get this job?\" [958] Michaelson paused before answering. \"I found it in the newspaper ad, sir.\" [959] \"Ah... since you've been working for Field for three years, Michaelson, you must know Benjamin Morgan, then?\" [960] Michaelson smiled politely. \"I certainly know Benjamin Morgan,\" he said earnestly. \"He's a very good man, sir. He's Field's partner, you know, in the legal field. But, about two years ago, they parted ways. I haven't seen Mr. Morgan since then.\" [961] \"Did you often see him before they parted?\" [962] \"No, sir.\" The sturdy butler replied with a tone of regret, \"Field is not Mr. Morgan's... type, you know, in terms of socializing. I remember seeing Mr. Morgan a few times in the apartment, but only when things were very urgent. Even then, I didn't know much, because I don't stay here at night... of course, as far as I know, Mr. Morgan hasn't come back since they dissolved the company.\" [963] Quine smiled for the first time during the conversation. \"Thank you for your honesty, Michael... I think I might become a nosy old man - do you remember if anything unpleasant happened after they parted ways?\" [964] \"Oh, no, sir!\" Michael asserted, \"I've never heard them argue or anything unpleasant. In fact, Field told me right after they dissolved the company that he and Morgan were still friends - very good friends.\" [965] Michael felt someone tap his arm and turned around politely, still with a blank expression. He found himself face to face with Ellery. \"What is it, sir?\" he respectfully asked. [966] \"Michaels, dear friend,\" Ellery said solemnly, \"I hate to bring up the past, but why haven't you told the police about your time in prison?\" [967] Michaels froze, his body stiff, unmoving, as the color drained from his face. He stared in shock at Ellery's smiling eyes, growing increasingly alarmed. [968] \"Ah\u2014ah\u2014how did you find out?\" The butler breathed heavily, his tone no longer so gentle and refined. Quinn looked approvingly at his son. Piggott and Johnson approached the trembling man. [969] Ellery lit a cigarette. \"I had no idea,\" he said cheerfully. \"Learning from the Oracle will be good for you, Michaels.\" [970] Michaels paled. He turned to Quinn, trembling. \"You\u2014you didn't ask me that, sir.\" He weakly defended himself. Then his tone became tense and bewildered again. \"And no one would want to tell the police such a thing...\"\n[971] \"Where did you serve your sentence, Michael?\" the officer asked kindly. [972] \"Emirilah Reformatory, sir,\" Michael mumbled. \"It was my first offense\u2014times were hard, I was hungry, and I stole some money\u2026 I didn't serve long, sir.\" [973] Quin stood up. \"Well, Michael, you should know that you're not a completely free man now. You can go home if you want, and you can find another job. But you have to stay where you are now and be available at any time\u2026\" He strode over to the black suitcase, opened it, and revealed a jumbled pile of clothes\u2014a dark suit, shirts, ties, socks\u2014some clean, some dirty. Quin quickly rummaged through the suitcase, closed it, and handed it to Michael. Michael stood by patiently, his face showing sadness. [974] \"I see you seem to have brought a lot of clothes, Michael,\" Quin said with a smile. \"I'm sorry, but you can't go on vacation now. That's just how life goes!\" [975] Michael murmured a \"goodbye\" and left with the suitcase. Piggott left the apartment shortly after. [976] Ellery leaned back and laughed heartily. \"What a gentleman! He's just making it up, Father... Do you think he's after something here?\" [977] \"He came here for something, obviously,\" the officer mused. \"That means there's something important here that we've overlooked... \"\n[978] He began to think when the phone rang. [979] \"Officer?\" Chief Inspector Wiley's deep voice came over the phone. \"I called the station, but you weren't there. I guess you're still at Field's... I got some interesting news from Brown Brothers' shop. Do you want me to go to Field's residence?\" [980] \"No need,\" Quinn replied. \"We're done here. I'll go to Field's law office on Money Street and then head back to the office. If anything important happens, I'll be there. Where are you now?\" [981] \"Fifth Avenue \u2013 I just left Brown Brothers.\" [982] \"Then go back to headquarters and wait for me. And, Thomas \u2013 send a police officer over right away.\" [983] Quine hung up the phone and turned to Johnson. [984] \"Stay here and wait for another officer \u2013 won't be long. Have him watch the apartment and arrange a relief, then report to headquarters... Come on, Euler. Today's going to be a busy day!\" [985] Euler's protests were in vain. His father hustled him out of the building and onto the street, where the roar of a taxi's exhaust effectively drowned out his objections. [986] Chapter 10: The Field Hat Shows Importance\n[987] At exactly 10:00 a.m., Quinn and his son pushed through the frosted-glass door with the inscription:\n[988] Montague Field\n[989] Attorney\n[990] They entered the spacious waiting room. The decor of the room, unsurprisingly, matched Field's taste in clothing. The room was empty. The inspector glanced around perplexed, then pushed open the door to the general office and stepped inside, with Ellery following behind. It was a rectangular room filled with tables, much like a newsroom, except for the rows of bookshelves stacked with thick legal tomes. [991]The room was in chaos: stenographers huddled in a small group, chattering excitedly; several male clerks whispered in corners; Hess was standing in the center of the room, engaged in conversation with a thin, pale, and melancholic man with gray hair. It was clear that Field's death had caused quite a stir in his law firm. [992]Upon seeing the Quinns enter, the employees looked at each other in surprise and began to quietly return to their desks. The room suddenly fell silent, making everyone feel awkward. Hess quickly approached them, his eyes red and weary. [993]\"Good morning, Hess,\" the detective said suddenly. \"Where is Field's private office?\" [994]The detective led them through the room to another office with the word \"Private\" written on the door. The three entered. [995]\"The guy had a thing for atmosphere, didn't he?\" Ellery chuckled, sinking into a red leather chair. [996] \"Let's hear it, Hessian,\" the officer said, taking a seat in a chair just like Ellery. [997] Hessian began to speak rapidly: \"When I arrived here last night, the door was locked. There was no light inside. I listened carefully but didn't hear any sounds. Therefore, I naturally assumed that no one was inside and spent the night in the corridor. At around eight forty-five this morning, the office manager rushed in, and I grabbed him. That's the tall one who spoke to me when you came in; his name is Oscar L\u00fcn.\" [998] \"An office manager, huh?\" the elderly man said, snorting a pinch of snuff. [999] \"Yes, sir. He either plays dumb or knows how to keep secrets well,\" Hessian continued. \"Of course, he had already seen the morning paper and was very sad about Field's death. I found that he didn't like the questions I asked... I didn't get anything out of him. He said he went straight home after work last night\u2014it seems Field left at around four o'clock and never returned\u2014he only found out about the murder from the newspaper. We've been here all morning, waiting for you to arrive.\" [1000] \"Call L\u00fcn over here.\" [1001] When Hixie returned, the tall, thin office manager followed behind him. Oscar Luin's appearance was not particularly likable. He was unusually thin, and his dark eyes looked shifty. His hooked nose and skeletal frame gave him a predatory look. The officer coolly scrutinized him. [1002] \"You're the office manager,\" he said. \"Well, what do you think about this, Luin?\" [1003] \"It's terrible, absolutely terrible,\" Luin sighed. \"I can't imagine how or why it happened. God, I was talking to him just four o'clock yesterday.\" [1004] \"Did he seem strange or worried when you were talking to Mr. Field?\" [1005] \"Not at all, sir,\" Luin answered nervously. \"In fact, he was in high spirits, joked about giants, and said he was going to see a very exciting play called 'The Gunfight.' I saw him mentioned in the newspaper where he was killed!\" [1006] \"Oh, did he mention that play to you?\" the policeman asked. \"Did he happen to mention who he was going with?\" [1007] \"No, sir,\" L\u00fcn shifted his feet. [1008] \"I see,\" Quine paused. \"L\u00fcn, as the manager, you should be more intimate with Mr. Field than other employees. How well do you know him?\" [1009] \"Nothing, sir, nothing at all,\" L\u00fcn hurriedly said. \"It's hard for employees to be close to Mr. Field. He occasionally talks about himself, but it's all general, joking, and not to be taken seriously. He's a generous and caring boss to us outsiders - that's it.\" [1010] \"To be precise, what kind of business does Mr. Field engage in, L\u00fcn? You must know something about that, right?\" [1011] \"Business?\" Loyne seemed surprised, \"Ah, the same as other law firms. I've only worked for him for about two years, but he has some high-profile clients, officer. I can give you a list of them...\"\n[1012] \"Good, send it to me,\" Quinn said, \"So, his business is thriving and well-respected, huh? Have you noticed any private visitors\u2014especially recently?\" [1013] \"No. I don't remember anyone else coming here except for his clients. Of course, he might have social interactions with some of them... Oh, right, his personal butler comes here occasionally\u2014that tall, muscular guy named Michael.\" [1014] \"Michael?\" the officer said thoughtfully. He looked up at Loyne. \"Alright, Loyne. That's all for now. You can let the employees go home today. And\u2014you can stay here for now. I think one of Simpson's men will be here soon, and he'll need your help.\" [1015] As soon as the door closed, Quinn stood up. \"Where is Field's private bathroom, Hess?\" he asked. The agent pointed to a door in the far corner of the room. [1016] Quin opened the door, and Ellery followed him inside. They looked at a small alcove in the corner of the room, which had a sink, a medicine cabinet, and a small closet. Quin first checked the medicine cabinet, which contained a bottle of iodine, a bottle of hydrogen peroxide, a tube of shaving cream, and other shaving supplies. \"There's nothing here,\" Ellery said. \"What did you find in the closet?\" [1017] \"What a hateful hat!\" the officer suddenly said angrily. Just then, there was a knock on the door, and Hessey opened it, letting in a young man with a pleasant face. [1018] \"Detective Quin?\" the newcomer politely asked. [1019] \"Yes,\" the officer said sternly. \"If you're a reporter, you can report that the police will arrest the killer of Field within twenty-four hours. That's all the information I can give you right now.\" [1020] The young man smiled slightly. \"I'm sorry, Detective, but I'm not a reporter. My name is Arthur Stoez, and I'm a newbie in the District Attorney Sampson's office. The prosecutor only contacted me this morning, and I've been busy with other things - that's why I'm late. The Field incident is terrible, isn't it?\" He smiled and threw his hat and coat onto the chair. [1021] \"Everyone thinks so,\" Quin grumbled. \"He certainly caused a lot of trouble. What instructions did Sampson give you?\" [1022] \"Well, I'm not very familiar with Field's profession, which is understandable since I'm only temporarily replacing Tim Croning, who is busy with other things this morning and couldn't be reached. I started working, and Tim will take over when he finishes his current task, which will be sometime this afternoon. Croning, you know, has been pursuing Field for years. He's eager to handle these records.\" [1023] \"Good. Based on what Sampson told me about Croning\u2014if there's anything incriminating in Field's records and files, Croning will surely find it. Hess, take Stouess to the outside and introduce him to Loyn\u2014he's the office manager, Stouess. Keep a close eye on Loyn\u2014he looks like a cunning fellow. And Stouess\u2014remember, you're not looking for legal business and clients in these records, but rather for any improper content\u2026 See you later.\" [1024] Stouess smiled at him and followed Hess out. Ellery and his father stood facing each other in the room. [1025] \"What are you holding?\" the old man asked sternly. [1026] \"A book called 'What Can Handwriting Tell Us', which I just took off the shelf,\" Ellery answered lazily, \"What's wrong?\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHe could have been a corporate executive or a night watchman, depending on his whim. Of course, with the right clothes, his unremarkable build could pass for any profession. [638] This adaptability was also evident in his demeanor. No one could truly understand him. He was a source of wonder for his colleagues, his enemies, and those wretches he handed over to the legal system. He could be any kind of personality, from mild-mannered to arrogant, from paternal to obstinate, depending on his whim. [639] However, deep down, as some more emotional people would argue, Quinn had a \"heart of gold.\" He had no malice in his heart, was warm-hearted, and was not affected by the cruel world. For those who dealt with him for business or official matters, he was a chameleon, and indeed he was. He always showed different aspects of his personality, as he found it good: people never understood him, never knew what he was going to say or do, so they always had a bit of fear of him. [640] At this moment, he returned to Pensel's office alone, closed the door, paused the investigation, and returned to his true self. At this moment, his face was wrinkled - physically old, but mentally wise. The image of the frightened, terrified girl kept flashing in his mind. Seeing her frail, terrified face made him want to escape. Francis Eves-Pope embodied everything an aging person would want their daughter to have. Seeing her fianc\u00e9 desperately defending her made him feel ashamed. [641] He sighed, reached for his snuff box, and took a deep drag. Although he was usually thrifty, he was willing to spend money on this. [642] There was a sudden knock at the door, and he immediately changed into an officer's demeanor - sitting at the desk, pondering the case. In fact, he hoped that Ellery would come in. [643] He said warmly, \"Come in.\" The door opened, and a thin, bright-eyed man in a heavy coat and a wool scarf walked in. [644] \"Henry!\" the officer called, standing up. \"I thought the doctor told you to stay in bed!\" [645] District Attorney Henry Sampson winked at Quinn and sat down in an armchair. [646] \"Doctor,\" he lectured, \"Doctors are so annoying. Now, tell me about the case. How's it going?\" [647] He groaned, carefully feeling his throat. Detective Queen sat down. [648] \"For an adult, Henry,\" he said firmly, \"you're the most uncooperative patient I've ever seen. You'll get pneumonia if you're not careful.\" [649] \"Well,\" the district attorney grinned, \"I have insurance, so I should worry... Oh, you haven't answered my question yet.\" [650] \"Oh, yes,\" Queen grumbled, \"your question. The case, you ask? It's going nowhere, my dear Henry. Is that satisfactory for you?\" [651] \"Please be more detailed,\" Henry said, \"remember, I'm a patient, my head is still buzzing.\" [652] \"Henry,\" Quine said, leaning forward solemnly, \"I'll tell you, this case is the most difficult one we've ever encountered in our department...Your head is buzzing, isn't it? I don't even want to talk about my head.\" [653] Henry frowned, looking at Detective Quine. \"If it's really as you say\u2014I think\u2014this is not a good time. The election is coming soon, an unsolved murder case, if it falls into the hands of the opposition... \"\n[654] \"Well, that's just one aspect,\" Quine whispered, \"I haven't considered the case from the election perspective, Henry. Someone was killed\u2014to be honest, I have no idea who killed him or how he was killed.\" [655] \"I accept your kind criticism, officer,\" Henry's tone lightened, \"but if you knew what I heard just a few minutes ago\u2014on the phone...\"\n[656] \"Wait a moment, my dear Watson\u2014as Ellery often says,\" Quin chuckled\u2014his sudden change in demeanor was typical of him, \"I bet I know what happened. You were at home, probably in bed. The phone rang, and then you heard complaints, protests, grumbling, and various sounds a person makes when agitated. The voice said: 'I can't believe I'm being treated like a criminal, being detained by the police. I want Quin to be severely punished! He's a serious threat to personal freedom!' and so on, right?\" [657] \"My dear fellow!\" Sampson smiled. [658] \"This gentleman, the one who complained to you,\u201d the officer continued, \u201cis a short, chubby man with gold-rimmed glasses, speaks like a woman, and has a very unpleasant voice. He also showed great concern for 'a very good friend, District Attorney Henry,' which is touching, isn't it?\" [659] Sampson sat there, staring at Quin. Then, a smile spread across his eager face. [660] \"It's shocking, my dear Sherlock!\" he whispered, \"Since you know so much about my friend, perhaps you can easily tell me his name?\" [661] \"Ah - is it this guy, right?\" Quine said, blushing, \"I - Ellery, my child! Nice to see you.\" [662] Ellery entered the room, shaking Sampson's hand warmly. Sampson greeted him enthusiastically, having worked with him for a long time. Ellery inquired about the District Attorney's condition and then set a large jar of coffee and a paper bag on the table, which could not help but make one think of French pastries inside. [663] \"Come on, gentlemen, the big search is finally over, done, even though the yield was small, the hardworking investigators now deserve a nightcap,\" he chuckled, patting his father's shoulder affectionately. [664] \"But, Ellery!\" Quine exclaimed happily, \"this is a great surprise! Henry, do you want to join us and celebrate a little?\" [665] \"I don't know what you're celebrating, but count me in,\" Sampson said. The three then enjoyed their meal together. [666] \"How's it going over there, Ellery?\" the old man asked, sipping his coffee contentedly. [667] \"Gods can go without eating or drinking,\" Ellery mumbled while eating a cream puff, \"I'm not all-powerful. So, tell me first what happened in your temporary interrogation room... but I can tell you something you don't know. Mr. Libby - the owner of the ice cream shop - confirmed what Jesse Lynch said about ginger ale, and Miss Eleanor Libby confirmed everything that happened in the alley.\" [668] Quinn wiped his mouth with a large handkerchief and said, \"Well, whatever, let Plautti check the ginger ale to make sure everything is fine. As for me, I just asked a few people, and now there's nothing else to do.\" [669] \"Thanks,\" Ellery said calmly, \"your statement is perfect! Have you told the district attorney about the events of this chaotic night?\" [670] \"Gentlemen,\" Sampson said, putting down his cup, \"I'll tell you what I've learned. About half an hour ago, a very good friend of mine called me - he happens to have some background - and clearly told me that someone was killed during tonight's performance. He accused you of violating the law, saying that Detective Richard Quinn rushed into the theater like a whirlwind, followed by the little whirlwinds, and made everyone wait for an hour - completely out of order, absolutely unforgivable. He also testified that Detective Quinn accused him of a crime and even ordered the overbearing police to search him, his wife, and daughter before they left the theater. [671] \"That's all the information my informant provided \u2013 the rest was just insults and complaints, not relevant to the case. The other thing is that Mr. Wiley just told me the identity of the victim. And, gentlemen, that's the most interesting part of the whole incident.\" [672] \"You know as much about this case as I do, Ellery,\" Quine grumbled, \"if not more, because I think you're quite familiar with Mr. Field's business activities... Ellery, what happened during the search outside?\" [673] Ellery comfortably crossed his legs. \"You probably guessed it, too, that the search of the audience didn't yield any results. We didn't find anything out of the ordinary. No one looked suspicious, and no one confessed. So, the search was a failure.\" [674] \"Of course, of course,\" Quine said, \"the person who planned this murder was very clever. I suppose you didn't see any suspicious extra hats?\" [675] \"This, Father,\" Ellery said, \"is why I'm here in the hall. No, there were no suspicious hats.\" [676] \"Is everything over over there?\" [677] \"I just finished buying pastries on the street,\" Ellery said, \"there was nothing going on over there either. They just had to make the angry audience line up and go out. Now, everyone's out, including the audience upstairs, the theater staff, and the cast\u2026 These actors are quite peculiar, they spend the whole night pretending to be God, pretending to be all-powerful; then suddenly they find themselves reduced to mere mortals, subject to the same troubles and diseases as everyone else. By the way, Willie searched the five people who left the office. The young lady has a very nice car. I guess it's Miss Evans-Pope and her friends\u2026 I don't know, but you probably forgot about them by now.\" [678] \"So, we're out of ideas, are we?\" Quine grumbled, \"The case is this.\" [679] \"That's it,\" Quine summarized after succinctly recounting what had happened in the small office, \"Now, Henry, you must have something to tell us about Monte Field. We know he's a very slick guy \u2014 but that's all we know about him.\" [680] \"You're being polite,\" Henry said harshly, \"I can recite his life story from memory. It seems you're in trouble now, and I think his past experiences might provide you with some clues.\" [681] \"Field was first investigated by our department during my predecessor's tenure,\" Henry continued. \"We suspected him of being involved in a scam involving an illegal syndicate. Assistant District Attorney Crowning was in charge of the case, but he couldn't catch Field on anything. Field's actions were very discreet. All we knew was what a snitch kicked out of the mob told us, but we couldn't verify the truth of it. Of course, Crowning never let Field know we suspected him. The case eventually cooled down, although Crowning persisted in his investigation, only to find nothing every time he thought he had something. Oh, you're right - Field is indeed cunning. [682] \"After I took office, on Crowning's strong recommendation, we conducted a detailed investigation into Field's background. Of course, it was done secretly. Our findings were that Monte Field was born into a New England aristocratic family, one that doesn't boast of being descendants of the Mayflower. He had private tutors as a child, then attended a prestigious private preparatory school, barely graduating. His father, clinging to a glimmer of hope, sent him to Harvard. Field seemed to be a bad egg from a young age. Although he didn't do anything illegal, his behavior was erratic. On the other hand, he had a strong sense of self-worth, so he shortened his last name to Field after a big argument with his father. [683] Quincannon and Ellery nodded. Ellery's eyes reflected with introspection, while Quincannon stared intently at Henry. [684] \"Field,\" Henry continued, \"is not entirely useless. On the contrary, he is very clever. He studied law at Harvard and excelled. He seems to have a talent for rhetoric, of course, thanks to his extensive legal knowledge. But just after graduation, before his family could enjoy any of the fruits of his education, he got involved in a dirty affair with a girl. His father immediately disinherited him. Then he was kicked out - he brought shame to the family - you know...\n[685] \"However, our friend was not defeated by misfortune. He used the little money he had left to venture out and make his own way. However, we don't know how he managed to survive during that time. Next, we learned that he had formed a partnership with a man named Cohen. Cohen was one of the most crooked lawyers around. What a partnership it was! They picked the biggest con artists in the gang and defended them. Through this, they made a lot of money. Now, you should understand how difficult it is to catch someone like that. They knew the loopholes in the law better than even the justices of the Supreme Court. They were not punished - it was a golden age for crime. The con artists felt they were a cut above the rest, as long as Cohen and Field defended them.\" [686] \"Among them, Cohen was experienced and knowledgeable, handling the 'contact' with the company's clients and determining the fees \u2013 despite his imperfect English, he did an excellent job \u2013 oh, and Cohen's fate was also tragic. He was murdered one winter evening, shot in the head. Although it has been twelve years, the killer has not been found. That is \u2013 from a legal standpoint, he has not been found. We have long suspected his identity. With Field's death tonight, I am not surprised that Cohen's case can be dropped. [687] \"So, he was just a playboy,\" Ellery murmured. \"Even after death, his face looks repulsive. I'm sorry I didn't buy the first edition of that novel.\" [688] \"Forget it, bookworm,\" Quin growled. \"Henry, continue.\" [689] \"Now \u2013\" Sampson picked up the last piece of cake from the table and savored it, \"now, let's talk about the good side of Field's life. After his partner's unfortunate death, he seemed to want to change and start anew. He started working \u2013 real legal work \u2013 and he was smart, always able to get things done. For several years, he worked alone, slowly erasing the stain on his reputation in the legal field, even occasionally gaining the respect of some of the proud bigwigs in the field. [690] \"This good performance \u2013 at least on the surface \u2013 lasted for six years. Then, he met Benjamin Morgan \u2013 a reliable, unblemished man with a good reputation, but lacking the talent to become a great lawyer. For some reason, Field convinced Morgan to work with him. After that, things got interesting.\" [691] \"You should remember that there were many illegal things happening in New York at that time, right? We vaguely felt that there was a huge criminal gang consisting of fence, con artists, and lawyers, with some politicians involved in some cases. There were several major robbery cases; bootlegging was rampant in the suburbs. Many people openly robbed and even killed, keeping the police busy. But we all knew that although you caught some of them, you didn't destroy the gang and didn't catch their leader. I have every reason to believe that our late friend, Monte Field, was the mastermind behind the whole thing. [692] \"With such a clever man, doing such things was a piece of cake. With Cohen's guidance, Monte Field had become very familiar with the brains of the entire underworld. After Cohen lost his utility value, he was killed offhand. Then, Field - remember, I'm mainly guessing here, because there's almost no evidence - then, Field openly began to engage in respectable legal affairs while quietly establishing a widespread criminal organization. Of course, we don't know how he did that. When everything was ready and prepared to do something big, he first teamed up with the respected and renowned Morgan, ensuring his legal status, and then began to plan the numerous major fraud cases of the past five years ...\n[693] \"What role did Morgan play in this?\" Ellery asked casually. [694] \"I was just about to talk about Morgan. We have every reason to believe that Morgan is absolutely innocent and has no connection with Field's secret criminal activities. He is a straight man, in fact, he often refuses to defend those with a bad reputation. When Morgan realized what was going on, their relationship must have been very tense. I don't know if that's the case - you can easily get an answer from Morgan. Anyway, they eventually went their separate ways. Since their partnership was dissolved, Field became more and more open, but we still had no concrete evidence and could not bring him to trial. [695] \"Sorry, I interrupt, Henry,\" Quin said thoughtfully, \"Can you provide more information about their breakup? Next time when questioning Morgan, I can also use it to verify what he says.\" [696] \"Of course, I can!\" Henry replied firmly. \"I'm glad you reminded me. Before they finally decided to part ways, they had a big argument that almost came to blows. They were having lunch at the Webster Club when they started arguing fiercely. The argument escalated, and eventually, someone had to intervene. Morgan was furious and even threatened to kill Field. I think Field must have been very calm at that time.\" [697] \"Did anyone witness why they argued?\" Quinny asked. [698] \"Unfortunately, no one knows. The situation quickly calmed down, and they parted ways peacefully. Since then, no one has heard anything about them.\" [699] After the district attorney finished speaking, the room fell into a contemplative silence. Ellery played a few notes of Schubert's music, and Quinny took a small pinch of snuff. [700] \"I would say, although I'm not entirely sure,\" Ellery said, looking up at the ceiling, \"that Morgan is now in big trouble.\" [701] His father muttered. Sampson said solemnly, \"Well, that's your business, gentlemen. I know what to do. Since Field is no longer around, I'll go through his files and records in detail. The rest doesn't matter. I just hope that after Field's murder, his whole gang will come crashing down. I'll send someone to his office tomorrow morning.\" [702] \"One of my men is already there,\" Quin said casually. \"So, you think it's Morgan, right?\" He asked, glancing at Ellery. [703] \"I seem to have already said it,\" Ellery said calmly. \"Basically, Morgan is in trouble now. There's nothing else. I admit, logically speaking, Morgan seems like the killer \u2013 except for one thing, gentlemen.\" [704] \"The hat,\" Quin said quickly. [705] \"No,\" Ellery said. \"Another hat.\" [706] Chapter 7: The Quinns Analyze the Case\n[707] \"Let's see where we stand now,\" Ellery continued. \"From the most basic angle, the facts are as follows: Monte Field was a notorious figure before his death, likely the head of a large criminal organization, and undoubtedly had many enemies. He was murdered at around 9:55 pm, just before the end of the second act at the Roman Theater. The person who discovered the case was named William Pusak, an employee with low intelligence. He was sitting in the same row as Field, five seats apart.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."